2768130391126510409742745975646712022148,0,0,0,28.892768130391126510409742745975646712022148\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Deposit release only against eligible expansion,28.892768130391126510409742745975646712022148,0E-43,28.892768130391126510409742745975646712022148,0,0,0,28.892768130391126510409742745975646712022148\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Maintenance and scheduled growth capex,28.892768130391126510409742745975646712022148,-1.1955843797295512371289563540600726418984181,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730,0,0,0,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Upfront scheduled capex GST with no cash refund assumed,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730,0.000000,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730,0,0,0,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Interest and term principal,27.697183750661575273280786391915574070123730,-0.16468252054794520547945205479452054794520548,27.532501230113630067801334337121053522178525,0,0,0,27.532501230113630067801334337121053522178525\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Remaining operating cash after payments,27.532501230113630067801334337121053522178525,7.0512385979539641179700423858504261429822388,34.583739828067594185771376722971479665160764,0,0,0,34.583739828067594185771376722971479665160764\\r\\noptional_rescue,bull,5,2031-10-20,Segregate continuation liquidity reserve before terminal valuation,34.583739828067594185771376722971479665160764,-7.0874850285051619469322187940455402036802406,27.496254799562432238839157928925939461480523,0,0,0,27.496254799562432238839157928925939461480523\\r\\n\"","\"# Quest Laboratories Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Research Hold. Do not treat the low reported P/E as investment clearance.** Quest is a pharmaceutical-formulations manufacturer, not Quest Flow, Quest Diagnostics or a diagnostic-laboratory operator. Its reported revenue has expanded substantially, and its first expansion phase is operating. However, the auditor could not verify inventory valuation in both FY25 and FY26 because sufficient records were not supplied. Inventory reached ₹38.62 crore, while cash from operations remained negative and receivables aged. These are central to whether the reported profit and assets are usable, not minor presentation issues. [FY26 annual report, printed pp42–49,52–75](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\nAt the 19 October 2026 close of ₹90.20, the verified current share count implies market capitalization of ₹147.81 crore and reported FY26 P/E of 9.94 times. Those numbers do not establish value when inventory, cash-flow comparatives and funding availability remain unresolved. BQS, GQS and IAS are **NS**, with explicit partial-score bounds rather than invented averages. The research is not eligible for numerical quality rankings and does not qualify as a compounder.\\n\\nThe strongest positive evidence is a long operating history, substantial nominal revenue growth, positive reported operating profit and a completed Phase I milestone. The strongest adverse evidence is the repeated inventory assurance limitation, persistent cash absorption, ₹6.54 crore of receivables aged two to three years, and an incomplete cash-to-entry bridge. A favorable conditional model cannot override those facts.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- **Issuer:** Quest Laboratories Limited; NSE Emerge symbol **QUESTLAB**; ISIN **INE0TNW01017**. Standalone issuer; no subsidiary, associate or joint venture identified in FY26.\\n- **Information cutoff:** 20 October 2026. **Market anchor:** 19 October 2026. Public records were retrieved on 23 October but only information published by the cutoff informs the decision. The April 2026 court decision was available before cutoff.\\n- **Financial anchor:** year ended 31 March 2026, latest September annual-report vintage. Earlier May results, original FY25/FY24 reports and prospectus restatements remain separate.\\n- **Horizon:** five years from 20 October 2026. **Equity hurdle:** 15%, with 12% and 18% tests. Nominal INR WACC range 13–17%, reflecting small scale, concentration, regulatory exposure and funding uncertainty. This is an analytical assumption, not a quoted financing rate.\\n- **Units:** ₹ crore unless stated; prices and EPS are rupees. Source statements in lakh or thousand are explicitly converted. No consolidated/standalone mixing.\\n- **Confidence:** identity and historical quoted price High; current tradability Medium; direction of cash weakness High; precise disputed cash-flow amounts Low; normalized profit, organic demand and actual entry liquidity Low. “ND” means not disclosed/not located; “NS” means not scoreable; “NM” means economically not meaningful.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Material stewardship and disclosure issues; no substantiated diversion and no current conviction inferred. |\\n| G2 | Hold | Two consecutive inventory assurance limitations and unresolved financial-statement inconsistencies exclude quality rankings. |\\n| G3 | Hold | Actual March-to-entry cash and deposit restrictions are unknown; optional rescue is not committed financing. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Official lot and 60 sessions verified, but discrete lot capacity, occasional inactivity and unverified current spread restrict implementation. |\\n\\nG1 is conditional rather than failed: reporting shortcomings and commercial related-party arrangements do not by themselves prove fraud. G3 is an unresolved hold, not a claim that actual insolvency has been demonstrated. Modeled funding failures occur under disclosed assumptions and cannot substitute for actual bank evidence. G2 is already sufficient to prevent a favorable investment classification.\\n\\nThe original FY21, FY22 and FY23 statutory opinions, CARO and IFC reports were examined, as were the complete FY24–FY26 audit suites and notes. Earlier unmodified reports did not contain the later inventory limitation. The FY24 IFC report refers to 31 December 2023, not 31 March 2024, so it is not represented as clean full-year IFC assurance. The source-safe audit register preserves the differences.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nThe actual v3.0 framework is used, with its original 13 factors, weights, gates, missingness and caps. No alternative “overall score” is created. Arithmetic uses Decimal precision, with conventional half-up rounding only for display. Original weights are not redistributed.\\n\\n| Measure | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw / final | Uncapped bound | Capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.5800 | 80.00% | NS / NS | 3.7800–5.5800 | 3.7800–5.5800 |\\n| GQS | 4.4000 | 70.00% | NS / NS | 4.7000–7.4000 | 4.7000–5.0000 |\\n| IAS | 1.0000 | 30.00% | NS / NS | 1.7000–8.0000 | 1.7000–8.0000 |\\n\\nFor each incomplete aggregate, the bound is known contribution plus missing weight multiplied by 1 or 10. It is a mathematical missing-data bound, not a confidence interval. The persistent CASH score of 2 caps GQS at 5; NS remains NS, not 5. No governance cap is applied at GOV 4. No funding cap is asserted from an unverified actual entry position. IAS uses BQS as NS, not its partial contribution or midpoint.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | BQS contribution | GQS contribution | IAS contribution |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | 0.9600 | 3.2000 | Not weighted |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | 0.5000 | 1.0000 | Not weighted |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | NS | NS | Not weighted |\\n| CASH | 2 | High on direction; Low on exact disputed amounts | 0.2000 | 0.2000 | Not weighted |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | NS | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | 0.3200 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | Not weighted | NS | Not weighted |\\n| R | NS | Low | Not weighted | Not weighted | NS |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Not weighted | Not weighted | 0.6000 |\\n| P | NS | Low | Not weighted | Not weighted | NS |\\n| L | 4 | Medium | Not weighted | Not weighted | 0.4000 |\\n\\n**GOV.** Repeated transparency and stewardship weaknesses, without sufficient evidence to allege diversion or score integrity at 3. E01 pp42–49 and E02 p50: inventory assurance limitation persists across two annual reports. E01 pp47–48,60,66–68,75: dues, proceeds, equity and related-party tables contain inconsistencies. E01 pp67–68: executive remuneration ₹1.32 crore, family salary ₹0.21 crore and controlled-party testing arrangement. **Counterevidence:** E12 quashed the historical conviction in April 2026; no current conviction or substantiated fraud may be inferred. No loan default reported in E01 CARO.\\n\\n**MOAT.** Established formulation capability and tender access, but no proven durable excess economics or pricing power. E04 business and risk sections: generic formulations and government procurement dependence. E05 slide11: management capacity/utilization claims are not an independently verified cost advantage. E08–E11: multiple batch-specific NSQ alerts complicate a qualification-based moat claim. **Counterevidence:** Long operating history, six formulation categories and existing government supply relationships provide genuine operating capability.\\n\\n**REV.** The preselected five-year nominal reported standalone revenue CAGR falls in the 20% to below 30% bracket; no trajectory adjustment. This is provisional on the declared reported basis, not verified organic demand. E04 restated FY21–23; E02 FY24 comparator; E01 FY25–26: no material perimeter change found, but organic constant-scope price/volume bridge is ND. Reported growth is 29.7881% over five intervals; three-year growth is shown separately. FY26 growth decelerated to 7.56%; one full year alone does not earn an additional trajectory deduction under the chosen policy. **Counterevidence:** Inventory and receivable reliability prevent treating this as validated cash-economic growth; G2 excludes ranking. The FY21 low base and subsequent product mix changes reduce extrapolation confidence.\\n\\n**DUR.** Essential generic-drug demand and existing market access offer runway, constrained by tender concentration, compliance and uncommissioned expansion. E04 p39: government sales 61.93% in FY23 and 77.91% in nine months to December 2023. E05: FY25 government mix approximately 62.51%, with a minor total-percentage discrepancy. E06: Phase I operational September 2026, Phase II machines expected December and trials January–February 2027. **Counterevidence:** Injection and export opportunities could broaden products and customers if registrations, qualification and collections are demonstrated.\\n\\n**CAP.** NS: audited inventory assurance and vintage/working-capital inconsistencies preclude a defensible sustainable incremental-return score. Accounting-proxy ROIC windows are disclosed, not promoted to project returns. E01: ₹38.6195 crore inventory, ₹36.2144 crore receivables and ₹1.4604 crore CWIP require economic recoverability evidence. **Counterevidence:** Reported operating profit increased materially over five years and the expansion may eventually raise asset utilization.\\n\\n**CASH.** Persistent poor operating cash realization and aged balances are directly evidenced. FY25–26 latest reported CFO totals negative ₹33.9343 crore despite positive PAT. E03 original FY24 CFO includes ₹1.630675 crore borrowing proceeds; adjusted conversion is weaker. E01 p58: ₹6.5402 crore trade receivables aged two to three years and conflicting over-six-month totals. **Counterevidence:** FY26 debt reduced materially and investment disposals funded the cash deficit; this is financing liquidity, not operating conversion.\\n\\n**BAL.** NS: actual entry liquidity and undrawn committed facilities cannot be established. E01 cash ₹0.1048 crore; ₹10.6469 crore deposits are not assumed unrestricted alongside unspent IPO capex obligations. Demand debt ₹6.5336 crore is callable; the financial-risk note repeats outstanding debt as undrawn facilities. Actual April–October bank utilization and proceeds bridge are unavailable. **Counterevidence:** Reported equity is substantial, debt declined and bank deposits could supply liquidity if availability and restrictions are proven.\\n\\n**EXEC.** Expansion remains partly unfinished, with delayed IPO utilization and unresolved reporting/compliance weaknesses. E01 p75: remaining IPO capex utilization extended through March 2027. E06: Phase I operating but Phase II not yet trialed or commissioned at cutoff. E05 capacity, daily output and sold-unit claims cannot be reconciled as one physical-capacity model. **Counterevidence:** Phase I commissioning is a concrete milestone and reported revenue has increased over the full history.\\n\\n**EARN.** NS: operating versus non-operating bridge is calculable, but sustainable per-share profit quality is not established. FY26 annual report differs from May results; FY25 comparatives differ from original annual report without a full reconciliation located. Inventory valuation directly affects cost and profit; tax benefits and investment income also influence PAT. **Counterevidence:** Excluding other income still leaves positive reported operating EBIT, rather than all profit coming from investments.\\n\\n**R.** NS: no supported five-year equity return can be assigned while actual entry liquidity and financial reliability remain unresolved. All modeled paths are explicitly conditional, with no ordinary IRR after a cash-floor breach. Optional rescue is bounded and not committed; it cannot cure G3 or supply a supported return score. **Counterevidence:** A favorable collection and commissioning path could produce positive returns, but assumptions are not evidence.\\n\\n**D.** Credible inventory/collection loss and recapitalization paths create material permanent-impairment risk. ₹38.6195 crore inventory lacks auditor verification; ₹6.5402 crore receivables are two to three years old. Demand refinancing and capex completion must coexist with very small disclosed cash. Bounded financing stress and capital catch-up are explicitly modeled. **Counterevidence:** Low reported financial leverage and deposits could cushion a verified adverse scenario; no insolvency is alleged.\\n\\n**P.** NS: headline P/E is observable but reliable normalized earnings and financeable reverse expectations are not. Current price is combined only with clearly dated historical earnings; stale balance-sheet EV is labeled a proxy. Reverse-model solutions, if any, remain conditional on liquidity, margins and terminal capital. **Counterevidence:** The reported earnings multiple is below 10 times, but this does not validate the earnings or the funding bridge.\\n\\n**L.** 60-session reference arithmetic starts at 5; deduct one point for intermittent trading and material lot granularity. Two issuer-absent sessions in validated official archives are conservatively assigned zero capacity, not independently verified zero trades. 19 October records one trade for one 1,200-share lot. ₹5 lakh reference position requires four or five whole lots; current spread and full surveillance history are ND. At 5% volume participation, 38 of 60 sessions cannot support a whole lot under the conservative zero-capacity convention. **Counterevidence:** The conservative mean/median method gives approximately 7.38 theoretical exit days at 5% participation, not an entirely inactive market.\\n\\nThe two most judgment-sensitive scored factors are GOV and MOAT. Each is perturbed by one point in canonical calculations. Moving GOV to 3 would require sufficient adverse evidence, trigger the governance cap on BQS and IAS, and change the action to Avoid — governance. It is not the current allegation. MOAT ±1 changes known BQS contribution by ±0.20 but leaves the G2 hold and incomplete aggregates unchanged. Score differences within these uncertainties should not be ranked.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nQuest manufactures generic pharmaceutical formulations at Pithampur, Madhya Pradesh. Its products include tablets, syrups, suspensions, ointments, ORS and other powders. It sells into government procurement and private channels. Government tender eligibility, product qualification, timely delivery and collection matter more to economics than a generic claim of a large pharmaceutical TAM. [IPO prospectus, business and risk sections](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf)\\n\\nGovernment customers accounted for 18.05% of revenue in FY21, 51.11% in FY22, 61.93% in FY23 and 77.91% in the nine months to December 2023. The July 2025 presentation puts FY25 government/private shares at 62.51%/37.29%, which sum to 99.80%; the discrepancy is retained rather than silently normalized. Latest FY26 government mix and top-customer shares were not established. Tender demand is recurring at an industry level, but individual tenders are not perpetual subscriptions and accounting receivables are not cash collection guarantees. [Prospectus p39](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf), [July 2025 presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf)\\n\\nReported FY26 product revenue was tablets ₹85.6947 crore, syrup ₹0.9158 crore, ointment ₹5.0325 crore, ORS ₹2.8671 crore and suspension ₹17.2982 crore. Product sales sum to ₹111.8083 crore, plus ₹0.0135 crore other operating revenue. Tablets represent 76.64% of product sales. Tablet revenue increased ₹18.9935 crore and suspension ₹8.0001 crore, while syrup, ointment and ORS fell. Total operating revenue increased only ₹7.8560 crore. The concentration and mix shift matter; “broad-based growth” is not supported by this table. [FY26 annual report, printed p63](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nThe demonstrated advantages are existing manufacturing capability, tender experience and customer access. These are useful but contestable. There is no verified evidence of a proprietary molecule portfolio, dominant consumer brand, durable pricing power, or an independently measured low-cost advantage. The relevant competition is other qualified generic-formulation suppliers, not diagnostic service chains.\\n\\nManagement reports annual capacity of 360 crore tablets, 60 lakh litres of liquids, 90,000 kg dry products, 2,700 tonnes ORS, 12 lakh litres external preparations and 432 tonnes ointments. FY25 utilization claims range from 25.42% for external preparations to 87.42% for liquids, with tablets at 77.92%. However, the same presentation's nine-million-tablet daily claim would require 400 operating days to reach 360 crore, and FY25 tablet sales volume of 1,475.1 million does not equal claimed utilization times capacity of 2,805.12 million. Sold and produced units can differ, but no complete reconciliation was located. These are management claims, not validated capacity economics. [Presentation slides10–11](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf)\\n\\nFour regulator records identify specific non-standard-quality batches: paracetamol suspension batch L-0475 in April 2024; duloxetine T26904 in December 2024; calcium/D3 QT23136 in April 2025; and ORS P0831 in June 2025. Findings include description, dissolution/assay and weight uniformity. These are batch-specific alerts, not a finding that every product is defective or that the entire plant is banned. Corrective-action closure, inspection history and current license conditions were not established. Certificates claimed by management must not be confused with a US FDA approval. [CDSCO April 2024](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20APRIL%202024.pdf), [December 2024](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20DEC-2024.pdf), [April 2025](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/CDSCO%20NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20APRIL-2025.pdf), [June 2025](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/STATE%20NSQ%20%20June%20-2025.pdf)\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\nReported standalone revenue rose from ₹30.3635 crore in FY21 to ₹111.8218 crore in FY26: **29.79% five-year CAGR**, versus **21.96% over three years**. Six observations support the five intervals. The five-year window was selected before scoring and produces REV 8, without a trajectory adjustment. It is nominal reported growth, not verified organic volume growth. No material acquisition or perimeter change was found, but a constant-scope price/volume/customer bridge is ND. The very low FY21 starting profit and subsequent product shifts make profit CAGR a poor stand-alone measure.\\n\\nFY26 revenue growth slowed to 7.56%, after approximately 25.92% in FY25 and 33.94% in FY24. One annual slowdown is not enough to claim a fully established new trend or make an extra arbitrary score deduction. It does materially constrain forecasts. Ten-year CAGR, latest comparable post-March TTM and post-March operating cash flow are ND; no seasonal quarter is annualized.\\n\\n| FY / basis | Revenue | Addition | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO | Cash capex printed | FCF printed | Debt | Cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 / Restated AS | 30.36 | NS | 1.42 | 0.66 | 1.05 | 1.21 | -0.16 | 3.04 | 0.42 |\\n| 2022 / Restated AS | 59.48 | 29.12 | 6.47 | 4.11 | 3.46 | 1.93 | 1.52 | 3.13 | 1.57 |\\n| 2023 / Restated AS; original comparative PAT ₹5.0449027 crore and CFO ₹3.8794338 crore differ | 61.64 | 2.16 | 7.82 | 5.03 | 3.63 | 3.07 | 0.56 | 4.10 | 2.50 |\\n| 2024 / Ind AS comparative, reconciliation to original AS incomplete | 82.56 | 20.92 | 15.56 | 10.09 | 3.01 | 0.94 | 2.07 | 5.08 | 0.14 |\\n| 2025 / Latest annual-report comparative; zero capex contradicts original347.13, not accepted as economic zero | 103.97 | 21.40 | 16.01 | 13.71 | -23.29 | 0.00 | -23.29 | 30.26 | 0.11 |\\n| 2026 / Ind AS; inventory assurance limitation and note discrepancies | 111.82 | 7.86 | 18.24 | 14.87 | -10.64 | 5.33 | -15.98 | 7.89 | 0.10 |\\n\\nThe FY21–23 row set is the prospectus's restated AS series. FY24 is the subsequent Ind AS comparative, FY25 the latest FY26 comparative, and FY26 the latest annual report. Original vintages are preserved separately. In particular, the FY25 printed capex zero is **not** accepted as economic zero: the original report showed ₹3.4713 crore cash capex. Accordingly, that year's printed FCF is not a reliable economic free-cash-flow measure. All headline EBITDA figures exclude reported other income through PBT + finance cost − other income + D&A. Gross profit is ND as a stable comparable reported measure; manufacturing/inventory classifications do not justify an invented gross margin.\\n\\n| FY | Bonus-adjusted or closing shares | Published EPS | Basis / limitation |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 0.61 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; not current-share EPS |\\n| 2022 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 3.81 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; not current-share EPS |\\n| 2023 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 4.66 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; original EPS46.76 was pre-bonus and not directly comparable |\\n| 2024 | 11937600 | original basic eps 9.11; later comparative eps 6.16 | E03 note7 and E02 EPS note: Original table labels11,937,600 weighted shares but EPS implies approximately11.08million; later comparative uses16,387,200. Weighted share bridge unreliable. |\\n| 2025 | 16387200 | original basic and diluted eps 8.28; later basic and diluted eps 8.37 | E02 EPS note; E01 p54: Published weighted shares16,387,200 for both FY25 and FY24 not consistent with actual issue timing; retain as published, do not infer EPS growth. |\\n| 2026 | 16387200 | basic and diluted eps 9.07 | E01 p54: Current full-year count, but earnings reliability limitations remain. |\\n\\nThe reported FCF proxy is CFO less cash capex, before financing-classified interest; it is neither FCFF nor distributable owner cash. Parent-attributable PAT equals standalone PAT in this perimeter. Recognized lease liabilities were not separately identified; leases are not silently estimated. Contract assets are ND rather than assumed to be verified zero.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe expansion has a real physical component, but the timing matters. The September 2026 filing says Phase I, a 12,000-square-foot building containing warehouse, blister packing and quality-control facilities, became operational on 16 September. Phase II construction was complete, but clean-room/utilities installation remained underway; machines were expected in December 2026, followed by trials in January–February 2027. This is not commercial commissioning of the injection line at the cutoff. [Dated expansion filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084144_NSE_Announcement_for_expansion.pdf)\\n\\nThe July 2025 presentation discussed ₹30–35 crore capex over three years, capsule capacity, new export registrations and a Myanmar order-book claim. None is modeled as certain contracted collections. The September strategic-advisory arrangement is a capability initiative, not booked revenue. The actual remaining capital commitment in Note28 was ₹0.6002 crore net of ₹0.51 crore advances, which is distinct from the much larger unused IPO object. [Presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf), [Advisory filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084828_Intimation_of_Strategic_Collabration.pdf), [Annual report, printed pp66,75](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n| FY | AR | Inventory | AP | Equity | IC broad proxy | IC reconciliation residual | NOPAT | Average ROIC proxy | Average AR days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 9.15 | 0.83 | 7.97 | 5.89 | 8.78 | -0.35 | 0.77 | NS | NS |\\n| 2022 | 22.06 | 1.09 | 17.05 | 9.99 | 11.92 | -0.46 | 4.49 | 43.42% | 95.77 |\\n| 2023 | 19.94 | 11.16 | 24.60 | 15.02 | 17.13 | -0.60 | 5.44 | 37.46% | 124.35 |\\n| 2024 | 24.44 | 16.12 | 20.63 | 28.67 | 30.91 | -4.06 | 11.22 | 46.71% | 98.10 |\\n| 2025 | 30.03 | 21.37 | 5.73 | 81.95 | 61.54 | -1.58 | 11.59 | 25.08% | 95.62 |\\n| 2026 | 36.21 | 38.62 | 5.61 | 96.82 | 88.95 | 0.13 | 13.04 | 17.32% | 108.12 |\\n\\nThe historical broad operating-capital proxy includes receivables, stock, other current/financial assets less operating-looking liabilities, plus PPE/CWIP. It deliberately exposes rather than hides classification uncertainty: tax and subsidy balances are not consistently separated across AS and Ind AS years. A financial-side proxy equals equity + debt − cash − investments. The displayed residual is not assumed to be goodwill or fixed by a plug. Current operating deposits are included once, not counted again as cash.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC proxy | Incremental ROIC proxy | Cumulative net investment | Bridge residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023–2026 | 7.59 | 71.82 | 10.57% | 69.74 | 2.08 |\\n| 2021–2026 | 12.27 | 80.18 | 15.30% | 78.19 | 1.99 |\\n\\nNormalized NOPAT uses operating EBIT and a 25.17% analytical tax rate. These incremental-return ratios are diagnostic accounting proxies, **not** project IRRs or evidence that new machinery earned those returns. They include working capital and CWIP, and the net-investment comparison includes cash capex less D&A plus change in broad ONWC. Residuals, revised financial vintages, inventory uncertainty and unreconciled noncash movements prevent a reliable CAP score. No unit-economics substitute was located. Returns should be tested against a 13–17% nominal cost-of-capital range, not compared casually with one fixed hurdle. A commissioned-assets-only view cannot be validated before Phase II trials and commercial sales.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nThe Sabarwal family controls the issuer. At 31 March 2026, disclosed promoter shares totaled 10,944,100, approximately 66.78%; the remainder is not necessarily executable free float. Reported executive remuneration was ₹1.32 crore, with ₹0.21 crore family salary. QAS Analytical Services is controlled by the promoter's son. The named testing transaction is ₹0.012 crore, but the aggregate analytical-expense table says NIL; a ₹0.2207 crore controlled-entity receivable similarly conflicts with a NIL total. These deserve reconciliation, not an unsupported allegation of diversion. [FY26 annual report, printed pp60,67–68](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n**Legal status must be current.** The IPO prospectus disclosed a January 2024 conviction and pending appeal involving a drug-quality case. The Jharkhand High Court's 20 April 2026 judgment in Criminal Appeal (SJ)116/2024 quashed the conviction and sentence because the company had not been made an accused, a necessary premise for the individual vicarious-liability prosecution. It is wrong to repeat the old conviction as current. The procedural/legal basis does not independently certify current product quality. The court-hosted original was not located; the reviewed source is a reproduction of the full judgment, with this limitation disclosed. Current status of other legacy cases is ND. [Reproduced judgment, 2026:JHHC:11417](https://indiankanoon.org/doc/105319622/), [IPO risk disclosures](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf)\\n\\nCapital allocation is mixed. The 9:1 bonus on 30 September 2023 was not economic dilution. A January 2024 cash allotment of 1,150,000 shares at ₹30 added ₹3.45 crore and increased shares from 10,787,600 to 11,937,600. The May 2024 IPO issued 4,449,600 shares at ₹97, gross ₹43.16112 crore, reaching 16,387,200 shares; existing holders' proportional ownership fell by approximately 27.15% if they did not participate. This dilution is separate from the bonus. Authorized capital supports 20 million shares, which is only a mathematical ceiling for rescue tests, not authority or a commitment to issue. [Prospectus capital history](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf), [FY26 annual report, printed pp59–60](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\nThe IPO capex object was ₹26 crore. Reported use through March 2026 was ₹8.1737 crore. Note40 gives remaining ₹17.8263 crore and an extension to 31 March 2027 approved on 13 February 2026. CARO instead prints ₹18.0621 crore unutilized, a ₹0.2358 crore arithmetic difference. The ₹10 crore working-capital object is shown fully used. These amounts are not added to cash again. The model uses the arithmetic-linked remaining capex object as a conditional budget, not a verified cash balance. The delayed timeline is an execution weakness despite Phase I progress.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n**Audit reliability.** FY25 and FY26 inventory valuation could not be verified for insufficient records, notwithstanding the unmodified headline opinions. FY26 CARO also could not opine on the adequacy of physical-inventory verification procedures. An “unmodified opinion” label does not erase that limitation. Inventory rose ₹17.2511 crore in FY26, far faster than revenue. Raw material ₹18.2228 crore, packaging ₹10.9329 crore, finished goods ₹5.1498 crore and WIP ₹4.3140 crore make the valuation and obsolescence question central.\\n\\n**Profit normalization.** FY26 reported PBT ₹17.9605 crore + finance ₹1.8538 crore − other income ₹2.3944 crore yields operating EBIT ₹17.4199 crore; adding D&A ₹0.8245 crore gives EBITDA ₹18.2444 crore. At 25.17% tax, operating NOPAT is ₹7.95 per current share before a financing deduction; it is not normalized equity EPS or a fair-price target. Reported PAT ₹14.8714 crore includes non-operating income and a deferred-tax benefit. The May results had PAT ₹15.0176 crore and PBT ₹18.1065 crore; the September report is lower. FY25 original PAT ₹13.5664 crore became ₹13.7124 crore in the later comparative. No complete reconciliation was located. The FY25 original EPS note uses 16,387,200 weighted shares even for FY24, despite the IPO occurring in FY25; those published EPS figures are not treated as a verified constant-share growth series.\\n\\n**Cash conversion.** Latest-vintage three-year CFO/PAT is -79.95%, and five-year is -49.85%, but exact values inherit the comparative inconsistencies. Original FY21 CFO ₹1.681218505 crore includes ₹0.239750187 crore borrowing inflow; a financing-classification-only adjustment gives ₹1.441468318 crore. Original FY22 CFO ₹3.149537 crore contains a negative ₹0.0955131 crore borrowing movement, so the analogous adjustment gives ₹3.2450501 crore; it also places ₹0.1805849 crore long-term borrowing in investing. These are not complete reconstructions, and original cash/deposit classifications differ from the prospectus. The direction is unambiguous: FY25 and FY26 absorbed cash while reporting profits. Original FY24 CFO of ₹3.0132542 crore included ₹1.6306750 crore borrowing inflow inside operating cash flow. Removing that financing produces ₹1.38 crore. FY23 original CFO similarly falls from ₹3.8794338 crore to ₹3.45 crore after removing ₹0.430032 crore financing. The later Ind AS presentation redistributes labels without establishing genuine collections. Using original FY24/FY25 and latest FY26 yields a clearly labeled mixed-vintage adjusted conversion of -84.57% and adjusted total-capex FCF of ₹-42.33 crore. This is a diagnostic, not an invented restatement. Security-deposit movements also appear in financing in the prospectus cash-flow presentation, limiting cross-vintage comparability.\\n\\nFY26's cash bridge is explicit: PBT ₹17.9605 crore plus D&A ₹0.8245 crore and finance ₹1.8538 crore, less interest income ₹2.2448 crore, investment gain ₹0.0005 crore and fair-value movement ₹0.1492 crore, gives operating profit before working capital of about ₹18.2443 crore. Receivables consumed ₹6.1827 crore, inventory ₹17.2511 crore, and other working-capital/tax-liability changes produced pre-tax cash of negative ₹6.0651 crore. Cash tax ₹4.5766 crore left CFO negative ₹10.6416 crore. Capex was an investing outflow of ₹5.3342 crore; it does **not** explain negative CFO. Investment disposals ₹37.6196 crore funded operating cash absorption, capex and debt reduction. CFI ₹34.8598 crore plus CFF negative ₹24.2263 crore reconcile the ₹0.0081 crore cash decline to ₹0.1048 crore.\\n\\nThe FY25 comparator does not reconcile: CFO −₹23.2927 crore + CFI −₹40.4980 crore + CFF ₹67.4285 crore implies a ₹3.6378 crore cash increase, while printed cash falls ₹0.0307 crore. The ₹3.6685 crore residual remains unresolved. The original FY25 cash flow also fails its opening/closing reconciliation, by approximately ₹0.1645 crore. No balancing plug is inserted.\\n\\n| FY | Manufacturing COGS proxy | Average AR days | Average inventory days | Average payable days | CCC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2025 | 80.54 | 95.62 | 84.95 | 59.72 | 120.86 |\\n| 2026 | 87.48 | 108.12 | 125.14 | 23.65 | 209.60 |\\n\\nThese days use average opening/closing balances. Inventory and payables use materials plus inventory change plus manufacturing expense as a compatible manufacturing-cost proxy. Credit purchases and credit-sales splits are ND, so payable days and total-revenue-based AR days are labeled proxies. The FY25 payable collapse is economically material, and low year-end debt does not establish good working-capital discipline.\\n\\n**Receivables and liabilities.** The over-six-month headline is ₹12.8387 crore, but the ageing buckets total only ₹10.4012 crore. The two-to-three-year bucket is ₹6.5402 crore, 18.06% of receivables. A policy asserting historically immaterial credit losses does not settle recoverability. CARO lists ₹0.140162 crore undisputed income-tax arrears for FY2018–19 and ₹0.3357 crore disputed for FY2019–20, contradicting a preceding no-arrears sentence; Note28 labels ₹0.4759 crore entirely disputed. The classification conflict is retained. No loan default or wilful-default designation was reported, but ₹5.6456 crore short-term funds were used for long-term purposes.\\n\\n**Liquidity is not the investment portfolio.** March cash was ₹0.1048 crore, debt ₹7.8853 crore, of which ₹6.5336 crore was demand debt. Bank deposits ₹10.6469 crore sat in current investments; listed equities ₹1.5341 crore and a zero-coupon CIPL NCD due2028 ₹3.3344 crore were separate. Deposit liens, availability and linkage to unspent IPO purposes were not confirmed. A note headed undrawn facilities repeats the outstanding debt figures; these are not counted as additional committed borrowing headroom. Gross debt less disclosed cash is ₹7.78 crore, not a declaration that all financial assets are unavailable. Both over-netting and treating all deposits as worthless would be unsupported.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n### Observable valuation, with date discipline\\n\\nMarket capitalization is ₹147.81 crore. Reported P/E is 9.94 times, current-share reported EPS ₹9.08, and reported book multiple 1.53 times. Current market capitalization plus March debt less March cash gives a **stale conservative EV proxy** of ₹155.59 crore, or 8.53 times reported operating EBITDA. It is not actual entry EV. Investments are not all netted without proving availability; neither are they counted again in terminal cash. Peer multiples are ND because a sufficiently comparable, reconciled peer set was not established. A superficial diagnostic-lab peer comparison would be wrong.\\n\\n### Linked cash and capital model\\n\\nThe model starts on 31 March 2026 and bridges to entry through 30 June, 30 September and 20 October, then uses 20 quarterly periods over five investment years. Period-end event timing is assumed, not a claim to know actual transaction dates. Pre-entry operations use an annual run-rate multiplied by exact elapsed days/365. Post-entry each anniversary year sums exactly to one, including the leap-year interval. All rows and cash events, including sensitivity and reverse-search rows, are in the canonical record.\\n\\nOpening ONWC is **₹70.5165 crore**: AR ₹36.2144 + inventory ₹38.6195 + supplier advances ₹1.8165 + prepaid expenses ₹0.0269 − trade payables ₹5.6089 − salary payable ₹0.2409 − customer advances ₹0.1695 − employee provisions ₹0.1415. Tax/government balances, subsidy receivable and financial investments are excluded. Adding PPE ₹14.8137, CWIP ₹1.4604 and operating deposits ₹0.6048 gives opening committed operating capital ₹87.3954 crore. Supplier advances remain in ONWC rather than being added again to capital commitments. The current trade-payable proxy excludes interest-bearing debt.\\n\\nOnly ₹0.1048 crore begins as ordinary cash. The ₹10.6469 crore deposit pool is assumed redeemable solely against eligible expansion payments, with a zero-availability stress. Opening statutory payable ₹0.3218 crore is settled in the first bridge period; legacy tax claims ₹0.4759 crore have a separate full-payment stress. Neither listed equities nor the NCD supplies routine cash or terminal value. No undrawn debt is assumed. Existing demand debt is assumed rolled in ordinary paths, explicitly stressed as non-rolled; the ₹1.3517 crore term balance amortizes daily to conservative quarter endpoints before the contractual March2027, December2027 and September2028 final maturities. The source current-year principal is ₹0.4423 crore; the modeled accelerated schedule is not the undisclosed actual EMI schedule. Annual interest is 10% of opening-period debt. These are analytical assumptions, not bank commitments.\\n\\nRemaining object capex ₹17.8263 crore is allocated 25% before entry and 75% in year1, with a 25% overrun in bear. Maintenance capex is 1.25 times D&A, not asserted equal to depreciation; 1.75 times is tested. Existing annual D&A is ₹0.8245 crore and new assets depreciate at 10%, tested at 18%. Upfront growth-capex GST is 18%, with no assumed cash refund. The cash floor is ₹0.10 crore. Only 25% of operating cash arrives before working-capital, capex, interest and principal payments; the rest arrives later. A zero-early-collections stress prevents annual netting from hiding intraperiod financing requirements.\\n\\n| Case | Growth | EBITDA margin | AR / inventory / AP days | Terminal revenue | Earned EV/EBIT | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Ownership retained |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2.00% | 10.00% | 155/210/18 | 104.94 | 1.86 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| base | 10.00% | 15.50% | 120/165/24 | 180.09 | 4.62 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| bull | 18.00% | 18.00% | 105/130/30 | 268.61 | 6.38 | NS | 15.26% | 233587 | 98.59% |\\n\\nBase assumes 10% revenue growth, 15.5% EBITDA margin, 120-day receivables, 165-day inventory and 24-day payables. Bear combines lower starting revenue, 2% growth, 10% margin, slower collections/stock, capex overrun and ₹6 crore cash loss. Bull needs 18% growth, 18% margin and materially better working capital. These are scenario assumptions, not forecasts justified by the published capacity claims. The model uses a compatible COGS ratio for inventory/payable days, not revenue as their denominator.\\n\\n| Case | First ordinary floor breach | Maximum ordinary gap | Optional rescue gross / fees | Rescue ceiling breached | Rescued exit price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2026-06-30 | 31.07 | 0.00 / 0.00 | False | NS |\\n| base | 2026-06-30 | 10.19 | 0.00 / 0.00 | False | NS |\\n| bull | 2028-01-20 | 1.63 | 1.69 / 0.05 | False | 183.51 |\\n\\n**No ordinary return survives an unfunded cash-floor breach.** Later negative-cash rows are retained only as counterfactual diagnostics. Optional rescue issues whole shares at ₹72.16, a fixed 20% discount to the market anchor, with 3% fees, ₹25 crore gross ceiling and no more than 3,612,800 new shares under the current authorized-capital ceiling. Each event rounds needed shares up and permitted shares down. Unfilled gaps suppress rescued returns too. Pre-entry rescue prohibition is distinguished from exhaustion of the financing ceiling; a separate bull-operating-case ₹40 crore post-entry cash-loss stress exercises the bounded-rescue ceiling rather than inventing funding beyond it. Rescue is uncommitted, requires approvals and investors, and never cures the actual-entry G3 hold. Pre-entry equity rescue is prohibited: a pre-entry cash breach irreversibly suppresses both ordinary and rescue returns. The verified entry share count is never replaced by invented pre-entry issuance. Post-entry rescue is available only to a path that has not already failed, and the terminal return is per existing ordinary share with subsequent dilution included. No investor is assumed to supply a pro-rata subscription without paying for it.\\n\\nAt the start of each growth year, before its first operating receipts, productive-capital needs are checked against run-rate NOPAT and the target sustainable capital return. Any catch-up capex, including its depreciation feedback and GST, is funded before receipts; deposited IPO funds are not diverted to this additional catch-up. At exit, productive capital must support normalized NOPAT at the case's sustainable terminal capital return, measured against closing productive capital rather than falsely labeled average annual ROIC. If insufficient, the model solves additional capex and its depreciation effect simultaneously, pays that capex and GST before valuation, and retests funding. CWIP noncommissioning is separately stressed. The basic growth-reinvestment claim is next NOPAT × growth/terminal ROIC. The model then imposes a floor equal to growth at the modeled working-capital and productive-fixed-asset intensity, adds maintenance capex above D&A and upfront GST on fixed growth investment, and deducts all of that from next NOPAT to obtain terminal FCFF. Thus the ROIC formula cannot underfund the physical capital path. Capitalized EV equals FCFF/(WACC − growth). Bear/base/bull use WACC 17%/15%/13%, growth2%/3%/4% and terminal ROIC10%/13%/18%. Before any exit value, the model builds a four-quarter continuation ledger and ring-fences its maximum prefix funding need. Growth fixed assets and GST are paid before growth receipts; all annual growth working capital is front-loaded into quarter1, while each quarter separately records early/late operating receipts, maintenance and interest. Every event must preserve the cash floor. A terminal path with negative annual net cash or nonpositive FCFF is not treated as self-funding and does not receive an ordinary or rescue return. The reserve is funded through the cash-event ledger and retested for breach or bounded rescue. Terminal equity deducts debt and adds only ordinary cash above the floor after segregation, so the operating reserve is not also counted as excess cash. There are no dividends. This is an exit-value model, not a full enterprise DCF, and **100% of investor value is terminal**, making the terminal-capital assumptions especially important.\\n\\n| Case / optional rescue | Productive capital | Required capital | Terminal catch-up | Growth reinvestment floor | Maintenance above DA | Continuation GST | Liquidity reserve | Terminal FCFF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 133.29 | 55.68 | 0.00 | 2.67 | 0.78 | 0.15 | 3.40 | 2.08 |\\n| base | 153.47 | 145.67 | 0.00 | 4.60 | 0.67 | 0.21 | 4.41 | 14.02 |\\n| bull | 186.18 | 185.23 | 0.00 | 7.71 | 0.99 | 0.41 | 7.09 | 25.57 |\\n\\n**Bear, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 52.86 | 5.29 | 1.05 | 11.27 | 6.42 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | -9.98 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 96.95 | 9.69 | 1.65 | 1.64 | 19.74 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -28.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 98.89 | 9.89 | 1.55 | 1.67 | 3.82 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | -26.69 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 100.87 | 10.09 | 1.61 | 1.70 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -24.38 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 102.88 | 10.29 | 1.66 | 1.74 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -21.95 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 104.94 | 10.49 | 1.71 | 1.77 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -22.80 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Base, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 62.19 | 9.64 | 2.16 | 1.19 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 3.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 123.00 | 19.07 | 4.09 | 7.17 | 16.00 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -2.17 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 135.30 | 20.97 | 4.45 | 7.89 | 3.26 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 2.28 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 148.83 | 23.07 | 4.99 | 8.68 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 7.77 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 163.72 | 25.38 | 5.57 | 9.54 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 14.13 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 180.09 | 27.91 | 6.21 | 10.50 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 17.01 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bull, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 65.30 | 11.75 | 2.69 | -11.17 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 17.55 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 138.55 | 24.94 | 5.53 | 10.68 | 17.68 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | 10.76 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 163.49 | 29.43 | 6.55 | 12.60 | 3.44 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 16.66 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 192.91 | 34.72 | 7.88 | 14.87 | 3.44 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 24.53 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 227.64 | 40.97 | 9.36 | 17.55 | 7.85 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 29.39 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 268.61 | 48.35 | 11.05 | 20.71 | 11.22 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 25.86 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bear, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 52.86 | 5.29 | 1.05 | 11.27 | 6.42 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | -9.98 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 96.95 | 9.69 | 1.65 | 1.64 | 19.74 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -28.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 98.89 | 9.89 | 1.55 | 1.67 | 3.82 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | -26.69 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 100.87 | 10.09 | 1.61 | 1.70 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -24.38 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 102.88 | 10.29 | 1.66 | 1.74 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -21.95 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 104.94 | 10.49 | 1.71 | 1.77 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -22.80 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Base, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 62.19 | 9.64 | 2.16 | 1.19 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 3.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 123.00 | 19.07 | 4.09 | 7.17 | 16.00 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -2.17 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 135.30 | 20.97 | 4.45 | 7.89 | 3.26 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 2.28 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 148.83 | 23.07 | 4.99 | 8.68 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 7.77 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 163.72 | 25.38 | 5.57 | 9.54 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 14.13 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 180.09 | 27.91 | 6.21 | 10.50 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 17.01 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bull, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 65.30 | 11.75 | 2.69 | -11.17 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 17.55 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 138.55 | 24.94 | 5.53 | 10.68 | 17.68 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | 10.76 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 163.49 | 29.43 | 6.55 | 12.60 | 3.44 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 18.30 | 16620787 |\\n| 3 | 192.91 | 34.72 | 7.88 | 14.87 | 3.44 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 26.17 | 16620787 |\\n| 4 | 227.64 | 40.97 | 9.36 | 17.55 | 7.85 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 31.03 | 16620787 |\\n| 5 | 268.61 | 48.35 | 11.05 | 20.71 | 11.22 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 27.50 | 16620787 |\\n\\nYear0 is the dated pre-entry bridge, not an investment year. Published annual summaries do not conceal the finer cash events. Negative post-breach balances are not permitted borrowing and do not receive a normal terminal return.\\n\\n### Sensitivities, hurdles and reverse expectations\\n\\n| Change | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Rescue ceiling exhausted |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Legacy tax claims settled | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| No early operating collections | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Deposits unavailable | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Demand debt not rolled | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Receivables 30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Inventory 60 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| EBITDA margin 3 points lower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| EBITDA margin 3 points higher | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Expansion capex 25% overrun | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Maintenance 1.75 times DA | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| New-asset DA 18% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| GST cash double | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Opening CWIP never productive | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| 25% aged AR and 10% stock loss | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| WACC 13% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| WACC 17% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Terminal growth 2% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Terminal growth 4% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Capital return 10% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Capital return 18% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Execution costs 1% entry, 5% exit | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Bull operating case, post-entry ₹40 crore cash-loss stress | NS | NS | 3464523 | True |\\n\\n| Growth | Margin | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 2.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 2.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Hurdle | Conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 12.00% | 104.13 |\\n| bull | True | 15.00% | 91.24 |\\n| bull | True | 18.00% | 80.21 |\\n\\nConditional entry values discount only financeable terminal claims at each hurdle; the rescue issue price is held fixed, so these are not self-consistent financing offers at a new market price. They are research diagnostics, not recommended buy thresholds.\\n\\n| Target | Rescue | Conditional growth roots | Search result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n\\nThe reverse search scans growth from −10% to80% in 2.5-point steps and solves only adjacent financeable brackets, checking funding throughout. It does not interpolate through an unfunded discontinuity or assume returns are monotonic. No bracket means no bracket within this disclosed search, not mathematical proof that no off-grid root exists. Full evaluated scan, bisection and solved paths are retained; roots failing the absolute 0.0000001 return/multiple residual tolerance are rejected. Even a numerical root is not a supported R score while G2 and actual entry funding remain unresolved.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe critical variable is **recoverability and verifiability of inventory and receivables**, not the revenue CAGR. A batch-quality problem or tender loss can reduce utilization and margin while stock remains unsold and government collections slow. Cash then falls while expansion payments and callable debt remain due. A discounted capital raise dilutes holders; if investors or lenders do not fund it, a normal exit valuation is unavailable. The separate stress charging 25% of two-to-three-year receivables plus 10% of inventory represents ₹5.4970 crore cash-equivalent loss, with no assumed tax shield. It is a scenario, not a forecasted provision or accusation that those balances are fictitious.\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument is the fall in debt, meaningful deposits, positive reported operating profit and Phase I progress. These could permit recovery if deposits are available and collections normalize. But investment disposals financing negative CFO are not proof the business now funds itself. The market may already discount these public issues; there is no evidence of a proprietary information advantage. The 2026 reversal of the historic conviction is an important favorable fact and prevents an overstated governance thesis.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\n**Speculative / unsupported at the cutoff.** Fivefold price appreciation over five years requires 37.97% annualized shareholder return; tenfold requires 58.49%. Enterprise growth is not the same as equity growth or per-share return: debt, recapitalization, margin, terminal capital needs and the earned multiple all intervene. The reverse tests above use the EV-to-equity bridge rather than a distorted historical EPS growth extrapolation. No precise probability is assigned.\\n\\nThe relevant revenue requirements are the fully modeled solved paths, if financeable, not a large industry TAM multiplied by an arbitrary market share. Current accessible-market share and independently verified Phase II commercial capacity are ND. Thus even a mathematical fivefold/tenfold root cannot be labeled plausible from evidence. The higher-WACC, lower-margin and lower-capital-return cases explicitly compress the earned terminal multiple. A defensible multibagger case would first require reliable cash economics, commissioned output and repeat orders collected without repeated dilution.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\nThis is a research checklist, not an automated monitoring commitment.\\n\\n| KPI or event | Baseline | Evidence needed / timing | Warning | Thesis-break evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Inventory assurance | FY25–26 insufficient records; ₹38.62 crore stock | Next audit and independent inventory/valuation reconciliation | Same limitation or rising stock without production/sales reconciliation | Verified materially unrecoverable or misstated stock without credible funding remedy |\\n| Collections | ₹36.21 crore AR; ₹6.54 crore two-to-three-year bucket | Next cash-flow filing, ageing and bank-supported receipts | Aged bucket persists or grows despite profit | Material collection failure causing unfunded obligations |\\n| Cash and bank access | ₹0.1048 crore cash; ₹10.6469 crore deposits | Dated entry-to-current bank, lien and sanction bridge | Deposit restrictions, demand repayment or no reliable bridge | Verified unfinanceable funding shortfall |\\n| IPO proceeds | ₹17.8263 crore remaining capex object; conflicting CARO figure | Reconciled use-of-proceeds and March2027 milestone | Further extension without scope/cost explanation | Misuse or loss of funds evidenced by reliable records |\\n| Phase II | Machinery expected December2026; trials January–February2027 | Installation, validation, licensing and first commercial collections | Milestone slip or cost overrun | Commercial qualification unavailable with stranded capital |\\n| Product quality | Four batch alerts across2024–25 | Regulator findings and corrective-action closure each material update | Repeat assay/dissolution failures | Material license restriction or pervasive verified control failure |\\n| Financial vintages | FY25/FY26 changes and nonreconciling cash flow | Issuer/auditor reconciliations | Unexplained revisions or continued contradictory tables | Reliable evidence accounts cannot support an investment valuation |\\n| Related parties | Testing/receivable aggregate mismatches | Reconciled schedules and settlement evidence | Growing unexplained related balances | Substantiated abusive diversion or minority harm |\\n| Liquidity | Two issuer-absent sessions;38/60 below one-lot participation capacity | Updated60-session exchange record before any implementation | Wider spreads, circuits or falling active days | Effectively unexecutable position |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Research Hold, no favorable investment classification and no ranking.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS. This is not a claim that every product or account is invalid, nor that the company is insolvent. It is a conclusion that critical evidence is not reliable enough for a supported entry recommendation.\\n\\nThe minimum path to reconsideration is: resolve inventory valuation and physical-verification limitations; reconcile financial vintages, cash flows and note inconsistencies; demonstrate actual unrestricted cash, deposit restrictions and committed bank headroom; show cash collections and Phase II commercial commissioning; and assess a lot-aware position against current execution conditions. A lower share price alone does not fix these evidence gaps. If severe abusive conduct is subsequently substantiated, classification would worsen rather than simply receive a smaller price target.\\n\\nAt ₹90.20, one 1,200-share lot costs ₹108,240. A ₹5 lakh reference is between four lots ₹432,960 and five lots ₹541,200, not the user's prescribed position. The 20-session mean/median daily traded value is ₹41.07438/₹22.88190 lakh. The 60-session zero-capacity-convention mean/median is ₹26.09607/₹13.55700 lakh; the two absent issuer rows are 30 July and 12 August. The observed-only 58-row median is ₹14.45220 lakh, implying about 6.92 days at 5%, in the same starting L band as the conservative 7.38-day estimate. Thus the provisional L 4 does not depend on pretending absence is a verified zero trade. Conservatively assigning zero capacity to two issuer-absent sessions in otherwise validated official archives and using the conservative 60-session median yields 7.38 theoretical exit days at 5% participation, versus 18.44 at 2% and 3.69 at 10%. However,38 of 60 sessions would not support even one whole lot at 5% of traded volume. The last session had one trade and one lot. Fractional-lot capacity is not executable; spread, queue priority, market impact and circuit locks can dominate these averages. G4 is conditional and L is 4, not an assurance of suitable liquidity. The current security master confirms the active SM series and lot; disabled alternative series are not mislabeled as an issuer suspension.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\nEvidence tags in the source register distinguish verified source statements from management claims, derived calculations and assumptions. All source financial claims remain attributed, including discrepancies. The model is analyst-owned analytical code, not issuer guidance. The archival files contain authored extracts, report, canonical record, model and offline rebuild/validation scripts, not redistributed source PDFs or OCR.\\n\\nOpen questions are specific: inventory valuation and count evidence; historical/corrected cash-flow reconciliation; actual April–October cash; current deposit liens and sanction limits; receivable confirmations; product-alert remediation and license status; exact tender/customer concentration; sustainable price/volume growth; Phase II production economics; current legacy-litigation status; and post-March interim cash flows. No external outreach has been sent.\\n\\nThe latest annual report is preferred for the headline FY26 values, but its reliability limitations remain. Earlier originals and May results are not overwritten. The reproduced court decision is explicitly lower provenance than a court-hosted original. Search/absence of a rating or restriction does not establish clearance. Historical prices and security records are exchange-sourced; they do not guarantee live fills.\\n\\n**E01. FY2026 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf). Printed pp12–22,42–82; complete statutory report, CARO and IFC read; financial image tables visually checked. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E02. FY2025 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_28424_QUESTLAB_2024_2025_A_3043270_04092025161607.pdf). Printed pp50–101 audit, CARO, IFC, statements and notes. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E03. FY2024 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_24894_QUESTLAB_2023_2024_090820241806.pdf). Printed pp52–77 audit, CARO, IFC, original AS statements; units INR hundreds. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E04. Final IPO prospectus**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf). pp30–58 risks,83–94 objects,128–161 business,221–239 litigation/approvals; F1–F46 restated accounts and annexures. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E05. Q1FY26 investor presentation**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf). Slides10–11 capacity/product mix;19 plans; management statements, not independent certification. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E06. Expansion update**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084144_NSE_Announcement_for_expansion.pdf). Document dated28 September; pp1–2. PhaseI operational16 September; PhaseII trial January–February2027 is expected. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E07. Strategic advisory collaboration**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084828_Intimation_of_Strategic_Collabration.pdf). pp1–3; advisory mandate is not a committed financing facility. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E08. April2024 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20APRIL%202024.pdf). p10 State Lab row16: paracetamol oral suspension L-0475, description. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E09. December2024 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20DEC-2024.pdf). Central Lab row43: duloxetine T26904, description/dissolution. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E10. April2025 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/CDSCO%20NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20APRIL-2025.pdf). pp1–2 row8: calcium+D3 QT23136, calcium dissolution/assay. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E11. June2025 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/STATE%20NSQ%20%20June%20-2025.pdf). Last page row130: ORS P0831, weight uniformity. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E12. Jharkhand High Court judgment reproduced by Indian Kanoon**. [Public source](https://indiankanoon.org/doc/105319622/). 2026:JHHC:11417, Criminal Appeal SJ116/2024, paragraphs8–13. Conviction and sentence set aside; court-hosted copy not retrieved. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E13. NSE official sixty-session bhavcopy series**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). Issuer-only extracted rows; each source URL/hash retained in market_extract.json;24 July–19 October2026. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E14. NSE security master**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). QUESTLAB SM row:16387200 shares,1200 lot, normal-market eligibility1; executable spread and surveillance list ND. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E15. Original FY2021 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report_2020-21.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; contrasted with prospectus restatement. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E16. Original FY2022 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report_2021-22.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; scanned. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E17. Original FY2023 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report%202022-23.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; original AS contrasted with restatement. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E18. FY26 results filing, earlier vintage**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29052026194528_outcome_of_bm-final.pdf). Earlier published PBT/PAT differ from September annual-report financial statements. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**Reproducibility:** all numeric model paths, including pre-entry periods, intraperiod events, bounded rescue, terminal-capital spending, sensitivity paths and reverse scans, are stored in the canonical JSON. Rebuilding from authored inputs does not read previously generated values or attestations. Validation rechecks accounting identities, whole-share ceilings, non-rescue return suppression, score arithmetic, market statistics, provenance and deterministic output hashes. The compact and full packages carry the same authored inputs; private transport receipts are excluded.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Research Hold. Do not treat the low reported P/E as investment clearance.** Quest is a pharmaceutical-formulations manufacturer, not Quest Flow, Quest Diagnostics or a diagnostic-laboratory operator. Its reported revenue has expanded substantially, and its first expansion phase is operating. However, the auditor could not verify inventory valuation in both FY25 and FY26 because sufficient records were not supplied. Inventory reached ₹38.62 crore, while cash from operations remained negative and receivables aged. These are central to whether the reported profit and assets are usable, not minor presentation issues. [FY26 annual report, printed pp42–49,52–75](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\nAt the 19 October 2026 close of ₹90.20, the verified current share count implies market capitalization of ₹147.81 crore and reported FY26 P/E of 9.94 times. Those numbers do not establish value when inventory, cash-flow comparatives and funding availability remain unresolved. BQS, GQS and IAS are **NS**, with explicit partial-score bounds rather than invented averages. The research is not eligible for numerical quality rankings and does not qualify as a compounder.\\n\\nThe strongest positive evidence is a long operating history, substantial nominal revenue growth, positive reported operating profit and a completed Phase I milestone. The strongest adverse evidence is the repeated inventory assurance limitation, persistent cash absorption, ₹6.54 crore of receivables aged two to three years, and an incomplete cash-to-entry bridge. A favorable conditional model cannot override those facts.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- **Issuer:** Quest Laboratories Limited; NSE Emerge symbol **QUESTLAB**; ISIN **INE0TNW01017**. Standalone issuer; no subsidiary, associate or joint venture identified in FY26.\\n- **Information cutoff:** 20 October 2026. **Market anchor:** 19 October 2026. Public records were retrieved on 23 October but only information published by the cutoff informs the decision. The April 2026 court decision was available before cutoff.\\n- **Financial anchor:** year ended 31 March 2026, latest September annual-report vintage. Earlier May results, original FY25/FY24 reports and prospectus restatements remain separate.\\n- **Horizon:** five years from 20 October 2026. **Equity hurdle:** 15%, with 12% and 18% tests. Nominal INR WACC range 13–17%, reflecting small scale, concentration, regulatory exposure and funding uncertainty. This is an analytical assumption, not a quoted financing rate.\\n- **Units:** ₹ crore unless stated; prices and EPS are rupees. Source statements in lakh or thousand are explicitly converted. No consolidated/standalone mixing.\\n- **Confidence:** identity and historical quoted price High; current tradability Medium; direction of cash weakness High; precise disputed cash-flow amounts Low; normalized profit, organic demand and actual entry liquidity Low. “ND” means not disclosed/not located; “NS” means not scoreable; “NM” means economically not meaningful.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Material stewardship and disclosure issues; no substantiated diversion and no current conviction inferred. |\\n| G2 | Hold | Two consecutive inventory assurance limitations and unresolved financial-statement inconsistencies exclude quality rankings. |\\n| G3 | Hold | Actual March-to-entry cash and deposit restrictions are unknown; optional rescue is not committed financing. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Official lot and 60 sessions verified, but discrete lot capacity, occasional inactivity and unverified current spread restrict implementation. |\\n\\nG1 is conditional rather than failed: reporting shortcomings and commercial related-party arrangements do not by themselves prove fraud. G3 is an unresolved hold, not a claim that actual insolvency has been demonstrated. Modeled funding failures occur under disclosed assumptions and cannot substitute for actual bank evidence. G2 is already sufficient to prevent a favorable investment classification.\\n\\nThe original FY21, FY22 and FY23 statutory opinions, CARO and IFC reports were examined, as were the complete FY24–FY26 audit suites and notes. Earlier unmodified reports did not contain the later inventory limitation. The FY24 IFC report refers to 31 December 2023, not 31 March 2024, so it is not represented as clean full-year IFC assurance. The source-safe audit register preserves the differences.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\nThe actual v3.0 framework is used, with its original 13 factors, weights, gates, missingness and caps. No alternative “overall score” is created. Arithmetic uses Decimal precision, with conventional half-up rounding only for display. Original weights are not redistributed.\\n\\n| Measure | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw / final | Uncapped bound | Capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.5800 | 80.00% | NS / NS | 3.7800–5.5800 | 3.7800–5.5800 |\\n| GQS | 4.4000 | 70.00% | NS / NS | 4.7000–7.4000 | 4.7000–5.0000 |\\n| IAS | 1.0000 | 30.00% | NS / NS | 1.7000–8.0000 | 1.7000–8.0000 |\\n\\nFor each incomplete aggregate, the bound is known contribution plus missing weight multiplied by 1 or 10. It is a mathematical missing-data bound, not a confidence interval. The persistent CASH score of 2 caps GQS at 5; NS remains NS, not 5. No governance cap is applied at GOV 4. No funding cap is asserted from an unverified actual entry position. IAS uses BQS as NS, not its partial contribution or midpoint.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | BQS contribution | GQS contribution | IAS contribution |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | 0.8000 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | 0.9600 | 3.2000 | Not weighted |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | 0.5000 | 1.0000 | Not weighted |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | NS | NS | Not weighted |\\n| CASH | 2 | High on direction; Low on exact disputed amounts | 0.2000 | 0.2000 | Not weighted |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | NS | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | 0.3200 | Not weighted | Not weighted |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | Not weighted | NS | Not weighted |\\n| R | NS | Low | Not weighted | Not weighted | NS |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Not weighted | Not weighted | 0.6000 |\\n| P | NS | Low | Not weighted | Not weighted | NS |\\n| L | 4 | Medium | Not weighted | Not weighted | 0.4000 |\\n\\n**GOV.** Repeated transparency and stewardship weaknesses, without sufficient evidence to allege diversion or score integrity at 3. E01 pp42–49 and E02 p50: inventory assurance limitation persists across two annual reports. E01 pp47–48,60,66–68,75: dues, proceeds, equity and related-party tables contain inconsistencies. E01 pp67–68: executive remuneration ₹1.32 crore, family salary ₹0.21 crore and controlled-party testing arrangement. **Counterevidence:** E12 quashed the historical conviction in April 2026; no current conviction or substantiated fraud may be inferred. No loan default reported in E01 CARO.\\n\\n**MOAT.** Established formulation capability and tender access, but no proven durable excess economics or pricing power. E04 business and risk sections: generic formulations and government procurement dependence. E05 slide11: management capacity/utilization claims are not an independently verified cost advantage. E08–E11: multiple batch-specific NSQ alerts complicate a qualification-based moat claim. **Counterevidence:** Long operating history, six formulation categories and existing government supply relationships provide genuine operating capability.\\n\\n**REV.** The preselected five-year nominal reported standalone revenue CAGR falls in the 20% to below 30% bracket; no trajectory adjustment. This is provisional on the declared reported basis, not verified organic demand. E04 restated FY21–23; E02 FY24 comparator; E01 FY25–26: no material perimeter change found, but organic constant-scope price/volume bridge is ND. Reported growth is 29.7881% over five intervals; three-year growth is shown separately. FY26 growth decelerated to 7.56%; one full year alone does not earn an additional trajectory deduction under the chosen policy. **Counterevidence:** Inventory and receivable reliability prevent treating this as validated cash-economic growth; G2 excludes ranking. The FY21 low base and subsequent product mix changes reduce extrapolation confidence.\\n\\n**DUR.** Essential generic-drug demand and existing market access offer runway, constrained by tender concentration, compliance and uncommissioned expansion. E04 p39: government sales 61.93% in FY23 and 77.91% in nine months to December 2023. E05: FY25 government mix approximately 62.51%, with a minor total-percentage discrepancy. E06: Phase I operational September 2026, Phase II machines expected December and trials January–February 2027. **Counterevidence:** Injection and export opportunities could broaden products and customers if registrations, qualification and collections are demonstrated.\\n\\n**CAP.** NS: audited inventory assurance and vintage/working-capital inconsistencies preclude a defensible sustainable incremental-return score. Accounting-proxy ROIC windows are disclosed, not promoted to project returns. E01: ₹38.6195 crore inventory, ₹36.2144 crore receivables and ₹1.4604 crore CWIP require economic recoverability evidence. **Counterevidence:** Reported operating profit increased materially over five years and the expansion may eventually raise asset utilization.\\n\\n**CASH.** Persistent poor operating cash realization and aged balances are directly evidenced. FY25–26 latest reported CFO totals negative ₹33.9343 crore despite positive PAT. E03 original FY24 CFO includes ₹1.630675 crore borrowing proceeds; adjusted conversion is weaker. E01 p58: ₹6.5402 crore trade receivables aged two to three years and conflicting over-six-month totals. **Counterevidence:** FY26 debt reduced materially and investment disposals funded the cash deficit; this is financing liquidity, not operating conversion.\\n\\n**BAL.** NS: actual entry liquidity and undrawn committed facilities cannot be established. E01 cash ₹0.1048 crore; ₹10.6469 crore deposits are not assumed unrestricted alongside unspent IPO capex obligations. Demand debt ₹6.5336 crore is callable; the financial-risk note repeats outstanding debt as undrawn facilities. Actual April–October bank utilization and proceeds bridge are unavailable. **Counterevidence:** Reported equity is substantial, debt declined and bank deposits could supply liquidity if availability and restrictions are proven.\\n\\n**EXEC.** Expansion remains partly unfinished, with delayed IPO utilization and unresolved reporting/compliance weaknesses. E01 p75: remaining IPO capex utilization extended through March 2027. E06: Phase I operating but Phase II not yet trialed or commissioned at cutoff. E05 capacity, daily output and sold-unit claims cannot be reconciled as one physical-capacity model. **Counterevidence:** Phase I commissioning is a concrete milestone and reported revenue has increased over the full history.\\n\\n**EARN.** NS: operating versus non-operating bridge is calculable, but sustainable per-share profit quality is not established. FY26 annual report differs from May results; FY25 comparatives differ from original annual report without a full reconciliation located. Inventory valuation directly affects cost and profit; tax benefits and investment income also influence PAT. **Counterevidence:** Excluding other income still leaves positive reported operating EBIT, rather than all profit coming from investments.\\n\\n**R.** NS: no supported five-year equity return can be assigned while actual entry liquidity and financial reliability remain unresolved. All modeled paths are explicitly conditional, with no ordinary IRR after a cash-floor breach. Optional rescue is bounded and not committed; it cannot cure G3 or supply a supported return score. **Counterevidence:** A favorable collection and commissioning path could produce positive returns, but assumptions are not evidence.\\n\\n**D.** Credible inventory/collection loss and recapitalization paths create material permanent-impairment risk. ₹38.6195 crore inventory lacks auditor verification; ₹6.5402 crore receivables are two to three years old. Demand refinancing and capex completion must coexist with very small disclosed cash. Bounded financing stress and capital catch-up are explicitly modeled. **Counterevidence:** Low reported financial leverage and deposits could cushion a verified adverse scenario; no insolvency is alleged.\\n\\n**P.** NS: headline P/E is observable but reliable normalized earnings and financeable reverse expectations are not. Current price is combined only with clearly dated historical earnings; stale balance-sheet EV is labeled a proxy. Reverse-model solutions, if any, remain conditional on liquidity, margins and terminal capital. **Counterevidence:** The reported earnings multiple is below 10 times, but this does not validate the earnings or the funding bridge.\\n\\n**L.** 60-session reference arithmetic starts at 5; deduct one point for intermittent trading and material lot granularity. Two issuer-absent sessions in validated official archives are conservatively assigned zero capacity, not independently verified zero trades. 19 October records one trade for one 1,200-share lot. ₹5 lakh reference position requires four or five whole lots; current spread and full surveillance history are ND. At 5% volume participation, 38 of 60 sessions cannot support a whole lot under the conservative zero-capacity convention. **Counterevidence:** The conservative mean/median method gives approximately 7.38 theoretical exit days at 5% participation, not an entirely inactive market.\\n\\nThe two most judgment-sensitive scored factors are GOV and MOAT. Each is perturbed by one point in canonical calculations. Moving GOV to 3 would require sufficient adverse evidence, trigger the governance cap on BQS and IAS, and change the action to Avoid — governance. It is not the current allegation. MOAT ±1 changes known BQS contribution by ±0.20 but leaves the G2 hold and incomplete aggregates unchanged. Score differences within these uncertainties should not be ranked.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nQuest manufactures generic pharmaceutical formulations at Pithampur, Madhya Pradesh. Its products include tablets, syrups, suspensions, ointments, ORS and other powders. It sells into government procurement and private channels. Government tender eligibility, product qualification, timely delivery and collection matter more to economics than a generic claim of a large pharmaceutical TAM. [IPO prospectus, business and risk sections](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf)\\n\\nGovernment customers accounted for 18.05% of revenue in FY21, 51.11% in FY22, 61.93% in FY23 and 77.91% in the nine months to December 2023. The July 2025 presentation puts FY25 government/private shares at 62.51%/37.29%, which sum to 99.80%; the discrepancy is retained rather than silently normalized. Latest FY26 government mix and top-customer shares were not established. Tender demand is recurring at an industry level, but individual tenders are not perpetual subscriptions and accounting receivables are not cash collection guarantees. [Prospectus p39](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf), [July 2025 presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf)\\n\\nReported FY26 product revenue was tablets ₹85.6947 crore, syrup ₹0.9158 crore, ointment ₹5.0325 crore, ORS ₹2.8671 crore and suspension ₹17.2982 crore. Product sales sum to ₹111.8083 crore, plus ₹0.0135 crore other operating revenue. Tablets represent 76.64% of product sales. Tablet revenue increased ₹18.9935 crore and suspension ₹8.0001 crore, while syrup, ointment and ORS fell. Total operating revenue increased only ₹7.8560 crore. The concentration and mix shift matter; “broad-based growth” is not supported by this table. [FY26 annual report, printed p63](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nThe demonstrated advantages are existing manufacturing capability, tender experience and customer access. These are useful but contestable. There is no verified evidence of a proprietary molecule portfolio, dominant consumer brand, durable pricing power, or an independently measured low-cost advantage. The relevant competition is other qualified generic-formulation suppliers, not diagnostic service chains.\\n\\nManagement reports annual capacity of 360 crore tablets, 60 lakh litres of liquids, 90,000 kg dry products, 2,700 tonnes ORS, 12 lakh litres external preparations and 432 tonnes ointments. FY25 utilization claims range from 25.42% for external preparations to 87.42% for liquids, with tablets at 77.92%. However, the same presentation's nine-million-tablet daily claim would require 400 operating days to reach 360 crore, and FY25 tablet sales volume of 1,475.1 million does not equal claimed utilization times capacity of 2,805.12 million. Sold and produced units can differ, but no complete reconciliation was located. These are management claims, not validated capacity economics. [Presentation slides10–11](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf)\\n\\nFour regulator records identify specific non-standard-quality batches: paracetamol suspension batch L-0475 in April 2024; duloxetine T26904 in December 2024; calcium/D3 QT23136 in April 2025; and ORS P0831 in June 2025. Findings include description, dissolution/assay and weight uniformity. These are batch-specific alerts, not a finding that every product is defective or that the entire plant is banned. Corrective-action closure, inspection history and current license conditions were not established. Certificates claimed by management must not be confused with a US FDA approval. [CDSCO April 2024](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20APRIL%202024.pdf), [December 2024](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20DEC-2024.pdf), [April 2025](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/CDSCO%20NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20APRIL-2025.pdf), [June 2025](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/STATE%20NSQ%20%20June%20-2025.pdf)\\n\\n\"","6","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\nReported standalone revenue rose from ₹30.3635 crore in FY21 to ₹111.8218 crore in FY26: **29.79% five-year CAGR**, versus **21.96% over three years**. Six observations support the five intervals. The five-year window was selected before scoring and produces REV 8, without a trajectory adjustment. It is nominal reported growth, not verified organic volume growth. No material acquisition or perimeter change was found, but a constant-scope price/volume/customer bridge is ND. The very low FY21 starting profit and subsequent product shifts make profit CAGR a poor stand-alone measure.\\n\\nFY26 revenue growth slowed to 7.56%, after approximately 25.92% in FY25 and 33.94% in FY24. One annual slowdown is not enough to claim a fully established new trend or make an extra arbitrary score deduction. It does materially constrain forecasts. Ten-year CAGR, latest comparable post-March TTM and post-March operating cash flow are ND; no seasonal quarter is annualized.\\n\\n| FY / basis | Revenue | Addition | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO | Cash capex printed | FCF printed | Debt | Cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 / Restated AS | 30.36 | NS | 1.42 | 0.66 | 1.05 | 1.21 | -0.16 | 3.04 | 0.42 |\\n| 2022 / Restated AS | 59.48 | 29.12 | 6.47 | 4.11 | 3.46 | 1.93 | 1.52 | 3.13 | 1.57 |\\n| 2023 / Restated AS; original comparative PAT ₹5.0449027 crore and CFO ₹3.8794338 crore differ | 61.64 | 2.16 | 7.82 | 5.03 | 3.63 | 3.07 | 0.56 | 4.10 | 2.50 |\\n| 2024 / Ind AS comparative, reconciliation to original AS incomplete | 82.56 | 20.92 | 15.56 | 10.09 | 3.01 | 0.94 | 2.07 | 5.08 | 0.14 |\\n| 2025 / Latest annual-report comparative; zero capex contradicts original347.13, not accepted as economic zero | 103.97 | 21.40 | 16.01 | 13.71 | -23.29 | 0.00 | -23.29 | 30.26 | 0.11 |\\n| 2026 / Ind AS; inventory assurance limitation and note discrepancies | 111.82 | 7.86 | 18.24 | 14.87 | -10.64 | 5.33 | -15.98 | 7.89 | 0.10 |\\n\\nThe FY21–23 row set is the prospectus's restated AS series. FY24 is the subsequent Ind AS comparative, FY25 the latest FY26 comparative, and FY26 the latest annual report. Original vintages are preserved separately. In particular, the FY25 printed capex zero is **not** accepted as economic zero: the original report showed ₹3.4713 crore cash capex. Accordingly, that year's printed FCF is not a reliable economic free-cash-flow measure. All headline EBITDA figures exclude reported other income through PBT + finance cost − other income + D&A. Gross profit is ND as a stable comparable reported measure; manufacturing/inventory classifications do not justify an invented gross margin.\\n\\n| FY | Bonus-adjusted or closing shares | Published EPS | Basis / limitation |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 0.61 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; not current-share EPS |\\n| 2022 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 3.81 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; not current-share EPS |\\n| 2023 | 10787600 | bonus adjusted eps 4.66 | E04 p197: Restated after 9:1 bonus; original EPS46.76 was pre-bonus and not directly comparable |\\n| 2024 | 11937600 | original basic eps 9.11; later comparative eps 6.16 | E03 note7 and E02 EPS note: Original table labels11,937,600 weighted shares but EPS implies approximately11.08million; later comparative uses16,387,200. Weighted share bridge unreliable. |\\n| 2025 | 16387200 | original basic and diluted eps 8.28; later basic and diluted eps 8.37 | E02 EPS note; E01 p54: Published weighted shares16,387,200 for both FY25 and FY24 not consistent with actual issue timing; retain as published, do not infer EPS growth. |\\n| 2026 | 16387200 | basic and diluted eps 9.07 | E01 p54: Current full-year count, but earnings reliability limitations remain. |\\n\\nThe reported FCF proxy is CFO less cash capex, before financing-classified interest; it is neither FCFF nor distributable owner cash. Parent-attributable PAT equals standalone PAT in this perimeter. Recognized lease liabilities were not separately identified; leases are not silently estimated. Contract assets are ND rather than assumed to be verified zero.\\n\\n\"","7","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nThe expansion has a real physical component, but the timing matters. The September 2026 filing says Phase I, a 12,000-square-foot building containing warehouse, blister packing and quality-control facilities, became operational on 16 September. Phase II construction was complete, but clean-room/utilities installation remained underway; machines were expected in December 2026, followed by trials in January–February 2027. This is not commercial commissioning of the injection line at the cutoff. [Dated expansion filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084144_NSE_Announcement_for_expansion.pdf)\\n\\nThe July 2025 presentation discussed ₹30–35 crore capex over three years, capsule capacity, new export registrations and a Myanmar order-book claim. None is modeled as certain contracted collections. The September strategic-advisory arrangement is a capability initiative, not booked revenue. The actual remaining capital commitment in Note28 was ₹0.6002 crore net of ₹0.51 crore advances, which is distinct from the much larger unused IPO object. [Presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf), [Advisory filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084828_Intimation_of_Strategic_Collabration.pdf), [Annual report, printed pp66,75](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n| FY | AR | Inventory | AP | Equity | IC broad proxy | IC reconciliation residual | NOPAT | Average ROIC proxy | Average AR days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 9.15 | 0.83 | 7.97 | 5.89 | 8.78 | -0.35 | 0.77 | NS | NS |\\n| 2022 | 22.06 | 1.09 | 17.05 | 9.99 | 11.92 | -0.46 | 4.49 | 43.42% | 95.77 |\\n| 2023 | 19.94 | 11.16 | 24.60 | 15.02 | 17.13 | -0.60 | 5.44 | 37.46% | 124.35 |\\n| 2024 | 24.44 | 16.12 | 20.63 | 28.67 | 30.91 | -4.06 | 11.22 | 46.71% | 98.10 |\\n| 2025 | 30.03 | 21.37 | 5.73 | 81.95 | 61.54 | -1.58 | 11.59 | 25.08% | 95.62 |\\n| 2026 | 36.21 | 38.62 | 5.61 | 96.82 | 88.95 | 0.13 | 13.04 | 17.32% | 108.12 |\\n\\nThe historical broad operating-capital proxy includes receivables, stock, other current/financial assets less operating-looking liabilities, plus PPE/CWIP. It deliberately exposes rather than hides classification uncertainty: tax and subsidy balances are not consistently separated across AS and Ind AS years. A financial-side proxy equals equity + debt − cash − investments. The displayed residual is not assumed to be goodwill or fixed by a plug. Current operating deposits are included once, not counted again as cash.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC proxy | Incremental ROIC proxy | Cumulative net investment | Bridge residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023–2026 | 7.59 | 71.82 | 10.57% | 69.74 | 2.08 |\\n| 2021–2026 | 12.27 | 80.18 | 15.30% | 78.19 | 1.99 |\\n\\nNormalized NOPAT uses operating EBIT and a 25.17% analytical tax rate. These incremental-return ratios are diagnostic accounting proxies, **not** project IRRs or evidence that new machinery earned those returns. They include working capital and CWIP, and the net-investment comparison includes cash capex less D&A plus change in broad ONWC. Residuals, revised financial vintages, inventory uncertainty and unreconciled noncash movements prevent a reliable CAP score. No unit-economics substitute was located. Returns should be tested against a 13–17% nominal cost-of-capital range, not compared casually with one fixed hurdle. A commissioned-assets-only view cannot be validated before Phase II trials and commercial sales.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nThe Sabarwal family controls the issuer. At 31 March 2026, disclosed promoter shares totaled 10,944,100, approximately 66.78%; the remainder is not necessarily executable free float. Reported executive remuneration was ₹1.32 crore, with ₹0.21 crore family salary. QAS Analytical Services is controlled by the promoter's son. The named testing transaction is ₹0.012 crore, but the aggregate analytical-expense table says NIL; a ₹0.2207 crore controlled-entity receivable similarly conflicts with a NIL total. These deserve reconciliation, not an unsupported allegation of diversion. [FY26 annual report, printed pp60,67–68](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\n**Legal status must be current.** The IPO prospectus disclosed a January 2024 conviction and pending appeal involving a drug-quality case. The Jharkhand High Court's 20 April 2026 judgment in Criminal Appeal (SJ)116/2024 quashed the conviction and sentence because the company had not been made an accused, a necessary premise for the individual vicarious-liability prosecution. It is wrong to repeat the old conviction as current. The procedural/legal basis does not independently certify current product quality. The court-hosted original was not located; the reviewed source is a reproduction of the full judgment, with this limitation disclosed. Current status of other legacy cases is ND. [Reproduced judgment, 2026:JHHC:11417](https://indiankanoon.org/doc/105319622/), [IPO risk disclosures](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf)\\n\\nCapital allocation is mixed. The 9:1 bonus on 30 September 2023 was not economic dilution. A January 2024 cash allotment of 1,150,000 shares at ₹30 added ₹3.45 crore and increased shares from 10,787,600 to 11,937,600. The May 2024 IPO issued 4,449,600 shares at ₹97, gross ₹43.16112 crore, reaching 16,387,200 shares; existing holders' proportional ownership fell by approximately 27.15% if they did not participate. This dilution is separate from the bonus. Authorized capital supports 20 million shares, which is only a mathematical ceiling for rescue tests, not authority or a commitment to issue. [Prospectus capital history](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf), [FY26 annual report, printed pp59–60](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf)\\n\\nThe IPO capex object was ₹26 crore. Reported use through March 2026 was ₹8.1737 crore. Note40 gives remaining ₹17.8263 crore and an extension to 31 March 2027 approved on 13 February 2026. CARO instead prints ₹18.0621 crore unutilized, a ₹0.2358 crore arithmetic difference. The ₹10 crore working-capital object is shown fully used. These amounts are not added to cash again. The model uses the arithmetic-linked remaining capex object as a conditional budget, not a verified cash balance. The delayed timeline is an execution weakness despite Phase I progress.\\n\\n\"","9","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n**Audit reliability.** FY25 and FY26 inventory valuation could not be verified for insufficient records, notwithstanding the unmodified headline opinions. FY26 CARO also could not opine on the adequacy of physical-inventory verification procedures. An “unmodified opinion” label does not erase that limitation. Inventory rose ₹17.2511 crore in FY26, far faster than revenue. Raw material ₹18.2228 crore, packaging ₹10.9329 crore, finished goods ₹5.1498 crore and WIP ₹4.3140 crore make the valuation and obsolescence question central.\\n\\n**Profit normalization.** FY26 reported PBT ₹17.9605 crore + finance ₹1.8538 crore − other income ₹2.3944 crore yields operating EBIT ₹17.4199 crore; adding D&A ₹0.8245 crore gives EBITDA ₹18.2444 crore. At 25.17% tax, operating NOPAT is ₹7.95 per current share before a financing deduction; it is not normalized equity EPS or a fair-price target. Reported PAT ₹14.8714 crore includes non-operating income and a deferred-tax benefit. The May results had PAT ₹15.0176 crore and PBT ₹18.1065 crore; the September report is lower. FY25 original PAT ₹13.5664 crore became ₹13.7124 crore in the later comparative. No complete reconciliation was located. The FY25 original EPS note uses 16,387,200 weighted shares even for FY24, despite the IPO occurring in FY25; those published EPS figures are not treated as a verified constant-share growth series.\\n\\n**Cash conversion.** Latest-vintage three-year CFO/PAT is -79.95%, and five-year is -49.85%, but exact values inherit the comparative inconsistencies. Original FY21 CFO ₹1.681218505 crore includes ₹0.239750187 crore borrowing inflow; a financing-classification-only adjustment gives ₹1.441468318 crore. Original FY22 CFO ₹3.149537 crore contains a negative ₹0.0955131 crore borrowing movement, so the analogous adjustment gives ₹3.2450501 crore; it also places ₹0.1805849 crore long-term borrowing in investing. These are not complete reconstructions, and original cash/deposit classifications differ from the prospectus. The direction is unambiguous: FY25 and FY26 absorbed cash while reporting profits. Original FY24 CFO of ₹3.0132542 crore included ₹1.6306750 crore borrowing inflow inside operating cash flow. Removing that financing produces ₹1.38 crore. FY23 original CFO similarly falls from ₹3.8794338 crore to ₹3.45 crore after removing ₹0.430032 crore financing. The later Ind AS presentation redistributes labels without establishing genuine collections. Using original FY24/FY25 and latest FY26 yields a clearly labeled mixed-vintage adjusted conversion of -84.57% and adjusted total-capex FCF of ₹-42.33 crore. This is a diagnostic, not an invented restatement. Security-deposit movements also appear in financing in the prospectus cash-flow presentation, limiting cross-vintage comparability.\\n\\nFY26's cash bridge is explicit: PBT ₹17.9605 crore plus D&A ₹0.8245 crore and finance ₹1.8538 crore, less interest income ₹2.2448 crore, investment gain ₹0.0005 crore and fair-value movement ₹0.1492 crore, gives operating profit before working capital of about ₹18.2443 crore. Receivables consumed ₹6.1827 crore, inventory ₹17.2511 crore, and other working-capital/tax-liability changes produced pre-tax cash of negative ₹6.0651 crore. Cash tax ₹4.5766 crore left CFO negative ₹10.6416 crore. Capex was an investing outflow of ₹5.3342 crore; it does **not** explain negative CFO. Investment disposals ₹37.6196 crore funded operating cash absorption, capex and debt reduction. CFI ₹34.8598 crore plus CFF negative ₹24.2263 crore reconcile the ₹0.0081 crore cash decline to ₹0.1048 crore.\\n\\nThe FY25 comparator does not reconcile: CFO −₹23.2927 crore + CFI −₹40.4980 crore + CFF ₹67.4285 crore implies a ₹3.6378 crore cash increase, while printed cash falls ₹0.0307 crore. The ₹3.6685 crore residual remains unresolved. The original FY25 cash flow also fails its opening/closing reconciliation, by approximately ₹0.1645 crore. No balancing plug is inserted.\\n\\n| FY | Manufacturing COGS proxy | Average AR days | Average inventory days | Average payable days | CCC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2025 | 80.54 | 95.62 | 84.95 | 59.72 | 120.86 |\\n| 2026 | 87.48 | 108.12 | 125.14 | 23.65 | 209.60 |\\n\\nThese days use average opening/closing balances. Inventory and payables use materials plus inventory change plus manufacturing expense as a compatible manufacturing-cost proxy. Credit purchases and credit-sales splits are ND, so payable days and total-revenue-based AR days are labeled proxies. The FY25 payable collapse is economically material, and low year-end debt does not establish good working-capital discipline.\\n\\n**Receivables and liabilities.** The over-six-month headline is ₹12.8387 crore, but the ageing buckets total only ₹10.4012 crore. The two-to-three-year bucket is ₹6.5402 crore, 18.06% of receivables. A policy asserting historically immaterial credit losses does not settle recoverability. CARO lists ₹0.140162 crore undisputed income-tax arrears for FY2018–19 and ₹0.3357 crore disputed for FY2019–20, contradicting a preceding no-arrears sentence; Note28 labels ₹0.4759 crore entirely disputed. The classification conflict is retained. No loan default or wilful-default designation was reported, but ₹5.6456 crore short-term funds were used for long-term purposes.\\n\\n**Liquidity is not the investment portfolio.** March cash was ₹0.1048 crore, debt ₹7.8853 crore, of which ₹6.5336 crore was demand debt. Bank deposits ₹10.6469 crore sat in current investments; listed equities ₹1.5341 crore and a zero-coupon CIPL NCD due2028 ₹3.3344 crore were separate. Deposit liens, availability and linkage to unspent IPO purposes were not confirmed. A note headed undrawn facilities repeats the outstanding debt figures; these are not counted as additional committed borrowing headroom. Gross debt less disclosed cash is ₹7.78 crore, not a declaration that all financial assets are unavailable. Both over-netting and treating all deposits as worthless would be unsupported.\\n\\n\"","10","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n### Observable valuation, with date discipline\\n\\nMarket capitalization is ₹147.81 crore. Reported P/E is 9.94 times, current-share reported EPS ₹9.08, and reported book multiple 1.53 times. Current market capitalization plus March debt less March cash gives a **stale conservative EV proxy** of ₹155.59 crore, or 8.53 times reported operating EBITDA. It is not actual entry EV. Investments are not all netted without proving availability; neither are they counted again in terminal cash. Peer multiples are ND because a sufficiently comparable, reconciled peer set was not established. A superficial diagnostic-lab peer comparison would be wrong.\\n\\n### Linked cash and capital model\\n\\nThe model starts on 31 March 2026 and bridges to entry through 30 June, 30 September and 20 October, then uses 20 quarterly periods over five investment years. Period-end event timing is assumed, not a claim to know actual transaction dates. Pre-entry operations use an annual run-rate multiplied by exact elapsed days/365. Post-entry each anniversary year sums exactly to one, including the leap-year interval. All rows and cash events, including sensitivity and reverse-search rows, are in the canonical record.\\n\\nOpening ONWC is **₹70.5165 crore**: AR ₹36.2144 + inventory ₹38.6195 + supplier advances ₹1.8165 + prepaid expenses ₹0.0269 − trade payables ₹5.6089 − salary payable ₹0.2409 − customer advances ₹0.1695 − employee provisions ₹0.1415. Tax/government balances, subsidy receivable and financial investments are excluded. Adding PPE ₹14.8137, CWIP ₹1.4604 and operating deposits ₹0.6048 gives opening committed operating capital ₹87.3954 crore. Supplier advances remain in ONWC rather than being added again to capital commitments. The current trade-payable proxy excludes interest-bearing debt.\\n\\nOnly ₹0.1048 crore begins as ordinary cash. The ₹10.6469 crore deposit pool is assumed redeemable solely against eligible expansion payments, with a zero-availability stress. Opening statutory payable ₹0.3218 crore is settled in the first bridge period; legacy tax claims ₹0.4759 crore have a separate full-payment stress. Neither listed equities nor the NCD supplies routine cash or terminal value. No undrawn debt is assumed. Existing demand debt is assumed rolled in ordinary paths, explicitly stressed as non-rolled; the ₹1.3517 crore term balance amortizes daily to conservative quarter endpoints before the contractual March2027, December2027 and September2028 final maturities. The source current-year principal is ₹0.4423 crore; the modeled accelerated schedule is not the undisclosed actual EMI schedule. Annual interest is 10% of opening-period debt. These are analytical assumptions, not bank commitments.\\n\\nRemaining object capex ₹17.8263 crore is allocated 25% before entry and 75% in year1, with a 25% overrun in bear. Maintenance capex is 1.25 times D&A, not asserted equal to depreciation; 1.75 times is tested. Existing annual D&A is ₹0.8245 crore and new assets depreciate at 10%, tested at 18%. Upfront growth-capex GST is 18%, with no assumed cash refund. The cash floor is ₹0.10 crore. Only 25% of operating cash arrives before working-capital, capex, interest and principal payments; the rest arrives later. A zero-early-collections stress prevents annual netting from hiding intraperiod financing requirements.\\n\\n| Case | Growth | EBITDA margin | AR / inventory / AP days | Terminal revenue | Earned EV/EBIT | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Ownership retained |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2.00% | 10.00% | 155/210/18 | 104.94 | 1.86 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| base | 10.00% | 15.50% | 120/165/24 | 180.09 | 4.62 | NS | NS | 0 | 100.00% |\\n| bull | 18.00% | 18.00% | 105/130/30 | 268.61 | 6.38 | NS | 15.26% | 233587 | 98.59% |\\n\\nBase assumes 10% revenue growth, 15.5% EBITDA margin, 120-day receivables, 165-day inventory and 24-day payables. Bear combines lower starting revenue, 2% growth, 10% margin, slower collections/stock, capex overrun and ₹6 crore cash loss. Bull needs 18% growth, 18% margin and materially better working capital. These are scenario assumptions, not forecasts justified by the published capacity claims. The model uses a compatible COGS ratio for inventory/payable days, not revenue as their denominator.\\n\\n| Case | First ordinary floor breach | Maximum ordinary gap | Optional rescue gross / fees | Rescue ceiling breached | Rescued exit price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 2026-06-30 | 31.07 | 0.00 / 0.00 | False | NS |\\n| base | 2026-06-30 | 10.19 | 0.00 / 0.00 | False | NS |\\n| bull | 2028-01-20 | 1.63 | 1.69 / 0.05 | False | 183.51 |\\n\\n**No ordinary return survives an unfunded cash-floor breach.** Later negative-cash rows are retained only as counterfactual diagnostics. Optional rescue issues whole shares at ₹72.16, a fixed 20% discount to the market anchor, with 3% fees, ₹25 crore gross ceiling and no more than 3,612,800 new shares under the current authorized-capital ceiling. Each event rounds needed shares up and permitted shares down. Unfilled gaps suppress rescued returns too. Pre-entry rescue prohibition is distinguished from exhaustion of the financing ceiling; a separate bull-operating-case ₹40 crore post-entry cash-loss stress exercises the bounded-rescue ceiling rather than inventing funding beyond it. Rescue is uncommitted, requires approvals and investors, and never cures the actual-entry G3 hold. Pre-entry equity rescue is prohibited: a pre-entry cash breach irreversibly suppresses both ordinary and rescue returns. The verified entry share count is never replaced by invented pre-entry issuance. Post-entry rescue is available only to a path that has not already failed, and the terminal return is per existing ordinary share with subsequent dilution included. No investor is assumed to supply a pro-rata subscription without paying for it.\\n\\nAt the start of each growth year, before its first operating receipts, productive-capital needs are checked against run-rate NOPAT and the target sustainable capital return. Any catch-up capex, including its depreciation feedback and GST, is funded before receipts; deposited IPO funds are not diverted to this additional catch-up. At exit, productive capital must support normalized NOPAT at the case's sustainable terminal capital return, measured against closing productive capital rather than falsely labeled average annual ROIC. If insufficient, the model solves additional capex and its depreciation effect simultaneously, pays that capex and GST before valuation, and retests funding. CWIP noncommissioning is separately stressed. The basic growth-reinvestment claim is next NOPAT × growth/terminal ROIC. The model then imposes a floor equal to growth at the modeled working-capital and productive-fixed-asset intensity, adds maintenance capex above D&A and upfront GST on fixed growth investment, and deducts all of that from next NOPAT to obtain terminal FCFF. Thus the ROIC formula cannot underfund the physical capital path. Capitalized EV equals FCFF/(WACC − growth). Bear/base/bull use WACC 17%/15%/13%, growth2%/3%/4% and terminal ROIC10%/13%/18%. Before any exit value, the model builds a four-quarter continuation ledger and ring-fences its maximum prefix funding need. Growth fixed assets and GST are paid before growth receipts; all annual growth working capital is front-loaded into quarter1, while each quarter separately records early/late operating receipts, maintenance and interest. Every event must preserve the cash floor. A terminal path with negative annual net cash or nonpositive FCFF is not treated as self-funding and does not receive an ordinary or rescue return. The reserve is funded through the cash-event ledger and retested for breach or bounded rescue. Terminal equity deducts debt and adds only ordinary cash above the floor after segregation, so the operating reserve is not also counted as excess cash. There are no dividends. This is an exit-value model, not a full enterprise DCF, and **100% of investor value is terminal**, making the terminal-capital assumptions especially important.\\n\\n| Case / optional rescue | Productive capital | Required capital | Terminal catch-up | Growth reinvestment floor | Maintenance above DA | Continuation GST | Liquidity reserve | Terminal FCFF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 133.29 | 55.68 | 0.00 | 2.67 | 0.78 | 0.15 | 3.40 | 2.08 |\\n| base | 153.47 | 145.67 | 0.00 | 4.60 | 0.67 | 0.21 | 4.41 | 14.02 |\\n| bull | 186.18 | 185.23 | 0.00 | 7.71 | 0.99 | 0.41 | 7.09 | 25.57 |\\n\\n**Bear, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 52.86 | 5.29 | 1.05 | 11.27 | 6.42 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | -9.98 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 96.95 | 9.69 | 1.65 | 1.64 | 19.74 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -28.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 98.89 | 9.89 | 1.55 | 1.67 | 3.82 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | -26.69 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 100.87 | 10.09 | 1.61 | 1.70 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -24.38 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 102.88 | 10.29 | 1.66 | 1.74 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -21.95 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 104.94 | 10.49 | 1.71 | 1.77 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -22.80 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Base, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 62.19 | 9.64 | 2.16 | 1.19 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 3.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 123.00 | 19.07 | 4.09 | 7.17 | 16.00 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -2.17 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 135.30 | 20.97 | 4.45 | 7.89 | 3.26 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 2.28 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 148.83 | 23.07 | 4.99 | 8.68 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 7.77 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 163.72 | 25.38 | 5.57 | 9.54 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 14.13 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 180.09 | 27.91 | 6.21 | 10.50 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 17.01 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bull, no new rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 65.30 | 11.75 | 2.69 | -11.17 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 17.55 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 138.55 | 24.94 | 5.53 | 10.68 | 17.68 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | 10.76 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 163.49 | 29.43 | 6.55 | 12.60 | 3.44 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 16.66 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 192.91 | 34.72 | 7.88 | 14.87 | 3.44 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 24.53 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 227.64 | 40.97 | 9.36 | 17.55 | 7.85 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 29.39 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 268.61 | 48.35 | 11.05 | 20.71 | 11.22 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 25.86 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bear, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 52.86 | 5.29 | 1.05 | 11.27 | 6.42 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | -9.98 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 96.95 | 9.69 | 1.65 | 1.64 | 19.74 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -28.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 98.89 | 9.89 | 1.55 | 1.67 | 3.82 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | -26.69 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 100.87 | 10.09 | 1.61 | 1.70 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -24.38 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 102.88 | 10.29 | 1.66 | 1.74 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -21.95 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 104.94 | 10.49 | 1.71 | 1.77 | 3.82 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | -22.80 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Base, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 62.19 | 9.64 | 2.16 | 1.19 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 3.61 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 123.00 | 19.07 | 4.09 | 7.17 | 16.00 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | -2.17 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 135.30 | 20.97 | 4.45 | 7.89 | 3.26 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 2.28 | 16387200 |\\n| 3 | 148.83 | 23.07 | 4.99 | 8.68 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 7.77 | 16387200 |\\n| 4 | 163.72 | 25.38 | 5.57 | 9.54 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 14.13 | 16387200 |\\n| 5 | 180.09 | 27.91 | 6.21 | 10.50 | 3.26 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 17.01 | 16387200 |\\n\\n**Bull, optional rescue**\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | Cash tax | ΔONWC | Capex | Interest | Principal | Debt close | Cash close | Shares close |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0 | 65.30 | 11.75 | 2.69 | -11.17 | 5.25 | 0.43 | 0.44 | 7.44 | 17.55 | 16387200 |\\n| 1 | 138.55 | 24.94 | 5.53 | 10.68 | 17.68 | 0.72 | 0.65 | 6.80 | 10.76 | 16387200 |\\n| 2 | 163.49 | 29.43 | 6.55 | 12.60 | 3.44 | 0.67 | 0.26 | 6.53 | 18.30 | 16620787 |\\n| 3 | 192.91 | 34.72 | 7.88 | 14.87 | 3.44 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 26.17 | 16620787 |\\n| 4 | 227.64 | 40.97 | 9.36 | 17.55 | 7.85 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 31.03 | 16620787 |\\n| 5 | 268.61 | 48.35 | 11.05 | 20.71 | 11.22 | 0.65 | 0.00 | 6.53 | 27.50 | 16620787 |\\n\\nYear0 is the dated pre-entry bridge, not an investment year. Published annual summaries do not conceal the finer cash events. Negative post-breach balances are not permitted borrowing and do not receive a normal terminal return.\\n\\n### Sensitivities, hurdles and reverse expectations\\n\\n| Change | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR | Rescue shares | Rescue ceiling exhausted |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Legacy tax claims settled | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| No early operating collections | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Deposits unavailable | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Demand debt not rolled | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Receivables 30 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Inventory 60 days slower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| EBITDA margin 3 points lower | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| EBITDA margin 3 points higher | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Expansion capex 25% overrun | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Maintenance 1.75 times DA | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| New-asset DA 18% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| GST cash double | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Opening CWIP never productive | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| 25% aged AR and 10% stock loss | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| WACC 13% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| WACC 17% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Terminal growth 2% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Terminal growth 4% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Capital return 10% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Capital return 18% | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Execution costs 1% entry, 5% exit | NS | NS | 0 | False |\\n| Bull operating case, post-entry ₹40 crore cash-loss stress | NS | NS | 3464523 | True |\\n\\n| Growth | Margin | Ordinary IRR | Optional rescue IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 2.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 2.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 10.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 12.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 15.50% | NS | NS |\\n| 18.00% | 18.50% | NS | NS |\\n\\n| Case | Rescue | Hurdle | Conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bear | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 12.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 15.00% | NS |\\n| base | True | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 12.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 15.00% | NS |\\n| bull | False | 18.00% | NS |\\n| bull | True | 12.00% | 104.13 |\\n| bull | True | 15.00% | 91.24 |\\n| bull | True | 18.00% | 80.21 |\\n\\nConditional entry values discount only financeable terminal claims at each hurdle; the rescue issue price is held fixed, so these are not self-consistent financing offers at a new market price. They are research diagnostics, not recommended buy thresholds.\\n\\n| Target | Rescue | Conditional growth roots | Search result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 0.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 15.00% | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 15.00% | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 5× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 5× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 10× | False | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n| 10× | True | NS | No adjacent financeable grid points bracket the target; not a proof no off-grid solution exists. |\\n\\nThe reverse search scans growth from −10% to80% in 2.5-point steps and solves only adjacent financeable brackets, checking funding throughout. It does not interpolate through an unfunded discontinuity or assume returns are monotonic. No bracket means no bracket within this disclosed search, not mathematical proof that no off-grid root exists. Full evaluated scan, bisection and solved paths are retained; roots failing the absolute 0.0000001 return/multiple residual tolerance are rejected. Even a numerical root is not a supported R score while G2 and actual entry funding remain unresolved.\\n\\n\"","11","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe critical variable is **recoverability and verifiability of inventory and receivables**, not the revenue CAGR. A batch-quality problem or tender loss can reduce utilization and margin while stock remains unsold and government collections slow. Cash then falls while expansion payments and callable debt remain due. A discounted capital raise dilutes holders; if investors or lenders do not fund it, a normal exit valuation is unavailable. The separate stress charging 25% of two-to-three-year receivables plus 10% of inventory represents ₹5.4970 crore cash-equivalent loss, with no assumed tax shield. It is a scenario, not a forecasted provision or accusation that those balances are fictitious.\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument is the fall in debt, meaningful deposits, positive reported operating profit and Phase I progress. These could permit recovery if deposits are available and collections normalize. But investment disposals financing negative CFO are not proof the business now funds itself. The market may already discount these public issues; there is no evidence of a proprietary information advantage. The 2026 reversal of the historic conviction is an important favorable fact and prevents an overstated governance thesis.\\n\\n\"","12","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\n**Speculative / unsupported at the cutoff.** Fivefold price appreciation over five years requires 37.97% annualized shareholder return; tenfold requires 58.49%. Enterprise growth is not the same as equity growth or per-share return: debt, recapitalization, margin, terminal capital needs and the earned multiple all intervene. The reverse tests above use the EV-to-equity bridge rather than a distorted historical EPS growth extrapolation. No precise probability is assigned.\\n\\nThe relevant revenue requirements are the fully modeled solved paths, if financeable, not a large industry TAM multiplied by an arbitrary market share. Current accessible-market share and independently verified Phase II commercial capacity are ND. Thus even a mathematical fivefold/tenfold root cannot be labeled plausible from evidence. The higher-WACC, lower-margin and lower-capital-return cases explicitly compress the earned terminal multiple. A defensible multibagger case would first require reliable cash economics, commissioned output and repeat orders collected without repeated dilution.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\nThis is a research checklist, not an automated monitoring commitment.\\n\\n| KPI or event | Baseline | Evidence needed / timing | Warning | Thesis-break evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Inventory assurance | FY25–26 insufficient records; ₹38.62 crore stock | Next audit and independent inventory/valuation reconciliation | Same limitation or rising stock without production/sales reconciliation | Verified materially unrecoverable or misstated stock without credible funding remedy |\\n| Collections | ₹36.21 crore AR; ₹6.54 crore two-to-three-year bucket | Next cash-flow filing, ageing and bank-supported receipts | Aged bucket persists or grows despite profit | Material collection failure causing unfunded obligations |\\n| Cash and bank access | ₹0.1048 crore cash; ₹10.6469 crore deposits | Dated entry-to-current bank, lien and sanction bridge | Deposit restrictions, demand repayment or no reliable bridge | Verified unfinanceable funding shortfall |\\n| IPO proceeds | ₹17.8263 crore remaining capex object; conflicting CARO figure | Reconciled use-of-proceeds and March2027 milestone | Further extension without scope/cost explanation | Misuse or loss of funds evidenced by reliable records |\\n| Phase II | Machinery expected December2026; trials January–February2027 | Installation, validation, licensing and first commercial collections | Milestone slip or cost overrun | Commercial qualification unavailable with stranded capital |\\n| Product quality | Four batch alerts across2024–25 | Regulator findings and corrective-action closure each material update | Repeat assay/dissolution failures | Material license restriction or pervasive verified control failure |\\n| Financial vintages | FY25/FY26 changes and nonreconciling cash flow | Issuer/auditor reconciliations | Unexplained revisions or continued contradictory tables | Reliable evidence accounts cannot support an investment valuation |\\n| Related parties | Testing/receivable aggregate mismatches | Reconciled schedules and settlement evidence | Growing unexplained related balances | Substantiated abusive diversion or minority harm |\\n| Liquidity | Two issuer-absent sessions;38/60 below one-lot participation capacity | Updated60-session exchange record before any implementation | Wider spreads, circuits or falling active days | Effectively unexecutable position |\\n\\n\"","14","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Research Hold, no favorable investment classification and no ranking.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS. This is not a claim that every product or account is invalid, nor that the company is insolvent. It is a conclusion that critical evidence is not reliable enough for a supported entry recommendation.\\n\\nThe minimum path to reconsideration is: resolve inventory valuation and physical-verification limitations; reconcile financial vintages, cash flows and note inconsistencies; demonstrate actual unrestricted cash, deposit restrictions and committed bank headroom; show cash collections and Phase II commercial commissioning; and assess a lot-aware position against current execution conditions. A lower share price alone does not fix these evidence gaps. If severe abusive conduct is subsequently substantiated, classification would worsen rather than simply receive a smaller price target.\\n\\nAt ₹90.20, one 1,200-share lot costs ₹108,240. A ₹5 lakh reference is between four lots ₹432,960 and five lots ₹541,200, not the user's prescribed position. The 20-session mean/median daily traded value is ₹41.07438/₹22.88190 lakh. The 60-session zero-capacity-convention mean/median is ₹26.09607/₹13.55700 lakh; the two absent issuer rows are 30 July and 12 August. The observed-only 58-row median is ₹14.45220 lakh, implying about 6.92 days at 5%, in the same starting L band as the conservative 7.38-day estimate. Thus the provisional L 4 does not depend on pretending absence is a verified zero trade. Conservatively assigning zero capacity to two issuer-absent sessions in otherwise validated official archives and using the conservative 60-session median yields 7.38 theoretical exit days at 5% participation, versus 18.44 at 2% and 3.69 at 10%. However,38 of 60 sessions would not support even one whole lot at 5% of traded volume. The last session had one trade and one lot. Fractional-lot capacity is not executable; spread, queue priority, market impact and circuit locks can dominate these averages. G4 is conditional and L is 4, not an assurance of suitable liquidity. The current security master confirms the active SM series and lot; disabled alternative series are not mislabeled as an issuer suspension.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\nEvidence tags in the source register distinguish verified source statements from management claims, derived calculations and assumptions. All source financial claims remain attributed, including discrepancies. The model is analyst-owned analytical code, not issuer guidance. The archival files contain authored extracts, report, canonical record, model and offline rebuild/validation scripts, not redistributed source PDFs or OCR.\\n\\nOpen questions are specific: inventory valuation and count evidence; historical/corrected cash-flow reconciliation; actual April–October cash; current deposit liens and sanction limits; receivable confirmations; product-alert remediation and license status; exact tender/customer concentration; sustainable price/volume growth; Phase II production economics; current legacy-litigation status; and post-March interim cash flows. No external outreach has been sent.\\n\\nThe latest annual report is preferred for the headline FY26 values, but its reliability limitations remain. Earlier originals and May results are not overwritten. The reproduced court decision is explicitly lower provenance than a court-hosted original. Search/absence of a rating or restriction does not establish clearance. Historical prices and security records are exchange-sourced; they do not guarantee live fills.\\n\\n**E01. FY2026 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31660_QUESTLAB_2025_2026_A_20351415_07092026114809.pdf). Printed pp12–22,42–82; complete statutory report, CARO and IFC read; financial image tables visually checked. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E02. FY2025 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_28424_QUESTLAB_2024_2025_A_3043270_04092025161607.pdf). Printed pp50–101 audit, CARO, IFC, statements and notes. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E03. FY2024 annual report**. [Public source](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_24894_QUESTLAB_2023_2024_090820241806.pdf). Printed pp52–77 audit, CARO, IFC, original AS statements; units INR hundreds. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E04. Final IPO prospectus**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Prospectus_QUEST.pdf). pp30–58 risks,83–94 objects,128–161 business,221–239 litigation/approvals; F1–F46 restated accounts and annexures. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E05. Q1FY26 investor presentation**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_25072025131320_Quest_Laboratories_Investor_Presentation_-_Q1_FY26-1_1.pdf). Slides10–11 capacity/product mix;19 plans; management statements, not independent certification. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E06. Expansion update**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084144_NSE_Announcement_for_expansion.pdf). Document dated28 September; pp1–2. PhaseI operational16 September; PhaseII trial January–February2027 is expected. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E07. Strategic advisory collaboration**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29092026084828_Intimation_of_Strategic_Collabration.pdf). pp1–3; advisory mandate is not a committed financing facility. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E08. April2024 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20APRIL%202024.pdf). p10 State Lab row16: paracetamol oral suspension L-0475, description. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E09. December2024 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20DEC-2024.pdf). Central Lab row43: duloxetine T26904, description/dissolution. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E10. April2025 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/CDSCO%20NSQ%20ALERT%20FOR%20THE%20MONTH%20OF%20APRIL-2025.pdf). pp1–2 row8: calcium+D3 QT23136, calcium dissolution/assay. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E11. June2025 regulator batch alert**. [Public source](https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadAlertsFiles/STATE%20NSQ%20%20June%20-2025.pdf). Last page row130: ORS P0831, weight uniformity. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E12. Jharkhand High Court judgment reproduced by Indian Kanoon**. [Public source](https://indiankanoon.org/doc/105319622/). 2026:JHHC:11417, Criminal Appeal SJ116/2024, paragraphs8–13. Conviction and sentence set aside; court-hosted copy not retrieved. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E13. NSE official sixty-session bhavcopy series**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). Issuer-only extracted rows; each source URL/hash retained in market_extract.json;24 July–19 October2026. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E14. NSE security master**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). QUESTLAB SM row:16387200 shares,1200 lot, normal-market eligibility1; executable spread and surveillance list ND. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E15. Original FY2021 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report_2020-21.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; contrasted with prospectus restatement. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E16. Original FY2022 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report_2021-22.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; scanned. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E17. Original FY2023 annual report**. [Public source](https://questlabltd.com/assets/images/pdf/Annual%20Report%202022-23.pdf). Original statutory report and financials; original AS contrasted with restatement. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**E18. FY26 results filing, earlier vintage**. [Public source](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/QUEST_29052026194528_outcome_of_bm-final.pdf). Earlier published PBT/PAT differ from September annual-report financial statements. Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\n**Reproducibility:** all numeric model paths, including pre-entry periods, intraperiod events, bounded rescue, terminal-capital spending, sensitivity paths and reverse scans, are stored in the canonical JSON. Rebuilding from authored inputs does not read previously generated values or attestations. Validation rechecks accounting identities, whole-share ceilings, non-rescue return suppression, score arithmetic, market statistics, provenance and deterministic output hashes. The compact and full packages carry the same authored inputs; private transport receipts are excluded.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","29","53","1727","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1776","4574","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4612","14326","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","14350","16301","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","16332","18712","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","18741","22856","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","22899","26544","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","26585","29586","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","29612","35599","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","35638","52978","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","53017","54259","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","54287","55365","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","55388","57730","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","57779","59989","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","60034","66598","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"b2353be9ee77084890de249f978bdc6831bca1bb91c4c7aaf504e1449ea8b584\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"63f9072dff9268e2bb430e7956d0322ec6c30d45a371fa8069ecf7f6d8e69561\"","\"Authored analytica